{"results":[{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI on-premises data gateway performance monitoring commercial tool","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\n- Currently, queries from premium capacity to the gateway are sometimes missed in this logging.\nWe are actively working on fixing this issue.\n- Currently, Power BI paginated report queries aren't logged using this tool.\n...\nThere are other values in the config file `C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config` that you can update as needed:\n- **ReportFilePath**: Determines the path where the four log files are stored.\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\nIf you use a service account for the gateway other than *PBIEgwService*, replace this part of the path with the service account name.\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n...\nThe default value is 5.\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nIf you're investigating slow-performing queries, in addition to using the performance monitoring feature, you can temporarily enable **Additional logging** to gather additional log information, including mashup engine logs, query strings, and verbose-level tracing.\nThese logs are written to the same place as your normal gateway logs.\nTo do this, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://3cloudsolutions.com/resources/monitoring-power-bi-on-premises-data-gateway-performance/","title":"Monitoring Power BI On-Premises Data Gateway Performance","domain":"3cloudsolutions.com","summary":"In this blog post we describe how to gather necessary gateway log data, and providing a detailed overview of the report we designed to leverage it.\nWe mention alternative options, and help you make sense of the data and tools made available by Microsoft.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft made Gateway Monitoring much easier via the addition of structured logs that can be enabled in the configuration file, and by providing a starter Gateway Performance Power BI report template to visualize the results.\nThese logs give full access to the information, minimizing the need to pull counter data from IT management systems, like the Microsoft System Center Operations Manager (SCOM); or run the Windows Performance Toolkit and dig through dust-covered gateway info and error log files; or pull refresh error messages from the Power BI Service.\n...\nAs of January 2020, there are four log files, and these are the main things you need to know to follow along:\n- **Query Execution **– Information for each individual query that was issued to an on-premises data source.\nIt also has data about a parent Request ID.\n...\nFor organizations with less complex or voluminous data, a process to copy all log files from each respective gateway machine to a central location (file system or a data lake) may be preferred over the more robust architecture defined below.\nHowever, in the long run, we recommend implementing a full-fledged solution to extract and store these files in a designated Power BI Monitoring Database, coupled  with additional Usage Auditing and Tenant Inventory data.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft released a report template, which is a great place to start to get data loaded and processed.\n...\n**The Gateway Performance Report template,** developed by BlueGranite, uses Microsoft’s as a starting point but expands and builds upon it to provide an actionable, easy-to-navigate, and use, report.\n...\nInstructions on how to set up Gateway Logging and the Microsoft Gateway Performance Power BI template download are available here.","date":"2020-02-11"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","summary":"I've been using the gateway and other performance monitor counters via PBI Desktop on a project recently.","date":"2019-06-07"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.\nThis is an amazing piece of work that is already being used by several very large customers.\n...\n1. This gets data from the gateway logs, the capacity metrics app shows data for CU usage","date":"2024-10-27"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\n{ts:381} solution that.\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\n…\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://community.dynamics.com/blogs/post/?postid=e6c22561-98ab-490d-9cc7-e757f0ae0a37","title":"Monitoring Microsoft On-Premise Data Gateway","domain":"community.dynamics.com","summary":"Let’s look into strategies you can adapt to avoid such scenarios:\n#### Preventive :i- Configure on-premise gateway service to start upon System Start: do this to ensure service is started in case VM is rebooted or updated or patched.\n- Service account: The Gateway service runs under an auto-created Virtual account but for finer control and management, you can switch it to use a managed Service account.\n- High Availability: For high availability, Install the On-premise Gateway in cluster Mode, clusters allow gateway administrators to group gateways to avoid single point of failure in accessing on-premises data resources\n#### Monitoring:r- Logs :\n- On-Premise Gateway Logs: All the on-premise gateway logs could be exported as a zip file from the on Premise Gateway Interface.\n- Additional Logs: Additional Logging could be enabled which is useful in for Performance diagnostics.\n- Pre-checks: Logic App and Flow integrations could be built to handle error for failure to connect using an on-premise gateway if the error occurs alerts could be sent to sysadmins.\n- Performance Counters: On-Premise Gateway has a list of performance counters which could be utilized for tracking and evaluating the performance of the gateway, It’s a larger topic so I would cover it in my next blog(Monitoring Microsoft On-Premise Data Gateway Part II)."},{"url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/PowerBI/comments/1h622ya/on_premises_data_gateway_report_question/","title":"On Premises data gateway Report Question : r/PowerBI - Reddit","domain":"www.reddit.com","summary":"1. [Service Gateway Performance](://learn.com/en/data-int/gateway/service-gateway-performance)\n2. [On-Premises Data Gateway Logs Template](://download.com/download/A/DA1DB86DA4F-B40-91E/OnisesDataLogs.pbit)\n...\nThe gateway functions as a \"tunnel\" connecting the Power BI service to the various machines or servers within your network that contain data.\nThe connection flow is as follows PBI <<-> Gateway <<> Data source Server.\nTo utilize data from all three servers, simply add their data sources to your report.\nIt’s sufficient to have the gateway installed on a single machine that can access all three servers from which you're extracting data.\nNotably, the gateway does not need to be installed on any of those three servers.\nAs long as it resides on a consistently accessible machine that can reach all three servers, you’re set.\nAdditionally, you can install the gateway on multiple machines, allowing them to distribute the workload and serve as backups to ensure high availability.\n...\nYou my preferred way is to copy/duplicate the data sources on the single pbit and change the source of the two copies to be the second and third gateway.","date":"2024-12-03"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7G8mwBVQJg","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway Monitoring Strategy. Why should I care?","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Obviously you don’t want to simply show the visuals frozen at the release state, but you want the underlying dataset to be refreshable so that the data visuals show up-to-date information.\nTherefore, after publishing the report on Power BI Service, you will certainly take care to install and configure an On-premises Data Gateway in Enterprise mode on a machine from which the data source is reachable, so that you can schedule a dataset refresh appropriately.\nWhenever you are dealing with a Data Gateway no matter how powerful your sources and Power BI capacity are, the Gateway is a crucial part of your architecture and you should be ready to react if problems arise.\nDuring this session we’ll see how to establish monitoring and troubleshooting strategy accordingly leveraging default logging and building a comprehensive solution.\n...\n{ts:253} so how we can solve this we simply put again our on-premises source on the left our services are on the right and we put something in the middle in the middle we put a software that is called on-premises data gateway and\n{ts:273} it's pretty simple the way it works it it talks with our software our sources and then thanks to an azure service bus instance it sends the data to uh the power bi service and in this way our\n…\n{ts:372} have to open any inbound ports when we have to um uh install an on-premises data gateway so you will find that we have two gateway types um we can install both standard and own personal mode gateways very easy to understand that the\n{ts:391} standard mode is something that is an enterprise solution so you are going to install this if you want to leverage your reports in an enterprise view and the personal mode instead it's something that it's more like\n{ts:409} uh it's more like uh something that you are going to use on your own um according to the different type of uh of gateway that you install you can use the gateway for specific applications so the personal mode only supports\n{ts:425} power bi the standard mode enables you to use it also for logic app for example power up and power automate and another difference is the standard mode obviously support all the storage mode so you can use it for\n...\n{ts:679} server is more free to do some work oh and obviously you have two different applications that you can uh use in order to understand uh which is the state of your gateways which is the level of patching and so on other other things\n{ts:698} that we are going to see something uh for example on the on-premise data gateway up and you have also some powershell uh command let's that you can use to manage okay so um we have done a very uh quick overview of\n...\n{ts:878} this is the first thing that we we could analyze then we can also analyze the volume of queries that we are supporting and most of the time we know that uh we have installed the power bi gateway and on-premise is that the gateway and we know there are some\n...\n{ts:1190} gives you a detailed information about the system resources that you have in a specific amount of time so you will set up the amount of time doing the aggregation and you will see which is the uh the results uh like cpu ram\n{ts:1211} allocated to the gateway service and so on in a specific moment and you will get also some specific uh report about query execution so we will get the list of query that had been run on on a specific gateway\n…\n{ts:1587} logging system and to be honest they did something uh if you go to this uh link with the qr code system you will uh you will land in this page where you can find a pbit uh\n{ts:1606} power power bi template file that you can simply download and point to the your custom location so you can simply extract the logs in a in a folder using maybe the export logs\n{ts:1623} from the um from the on-premises app and you can point to them using this template the pros of this solution is that it's obviously free because microsoft supports this on their docs\n{ts:1642} and gives you the possibility to download without any fee it's very quick to install because you download it and you open without any problem it's a power bi template thank you very much uh olivia for putting the link in the chat\n{ts:1660} and then uh it's uh obviously uh 100 compatible with all the power bi gateways so you can download this power bi template and use it on all your customer because it's 100 compatible it uses only the logs that you can extract from any\n{ts:1681} gateway it by default it allows to get some insights from the logging because it picks up the log files and then gives you some report out of the box some data had already been prepared in\n{ts:1698} some way you have some uh some measures some data application or something like that some preparation is already there and probably you can give insights to power bi teams without the need of\n...\n{ts:1961} leverage this solution so we started from that idea getting the data from the the standard login files trying to add some values on top of that uh leveraging the standard solution so the first idea was to\n{ts:1979} create pages uh create a power bi report that shows the performance counter of our gateway machine by topic so we divided the cpu informations in a specific page\n{ts:1996} we presented the ram counters in another page and we presented the disks counter in a in another page so the user can easily understand which is the counter is analyzing and which is um and you can see a little bit better the\n...\n{ts:4465} let's say analyzing tool or monitoring tool um let's see you have you have something that monitors the resource of your gateway for example so you can you can use the vmware um vmware applications that does for more or less","date":"2022-02-09"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI gateway monitoring solution vendor enterprise","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features/gateway-monitoring","title":"Power BI Gateway Monitoring | SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"Gateway Monitoring\n...\n# Spot personal gateways and connectivity risks before they cause failures.\n...\n- Every gateway and connection cataloged, including personal ones\n- Online/offline monitoring with version tracking\n- Dependency mapping shows which datasets are at risk\n...\n## What you get in SummitView\nTenant-wide gateway inventory\nenterprise and personal\nOnline/offline status monitoring with alerting\nPersonal gateway connection flagging with governance risk indicators\nData source count and type tracking per gateway\nVersion tracking with outdated-version warnings\nDependency mapping\nsee which datasets depend on which gateways\nAffected-dataset context in every alert\nknow the blast radius instantly\n...\nConnect via Cloud Connect or the optional agent — SummitView enumerates all gateways and data sources automatically.\n...\nGateway health, version, and type are monitored continuously.\n3\nPersonal gateways and offline gateways trigger alerts with affected-dataset context.\n...\nNo matter your Power BI license, gateway monitoring is included.\nPower BI Pro\nCore monitoring, usage tracking, alerting\nPremium Per User\n...\nGateway Monitoring works with all Power BI license types.\nIt monitors gateways at the tenant level — both enterprise gateways managed by IT and personal gateways installed by individual users.\n...\nStart your 14-day free trial.\n...\nSet up in 5 minutes.\n$299/mo per tenant","date":"2026-02-01"},{"url":"https://3cloudsolutions.com/resources/monitoring-power-bi-on-premises-data-gateway-performance/","title":"Monitoring Power BI On-Premises Data Gateway Performance","domain":"3cloudsolutions.com","summary":"In June 2019, Microsoft made Gateway Monitoring much easier via the addition of structured logs that can be enabled in the configuration file, and by providing a starter Gateway Performance Power BI report template to visualize the results.\n...\nHowever, in the long run, we recommend implementing a full-fledged solution to extract and store these files in a designated Power BI Monitoring Database, coupled  with additional Usage Auditing and Tenant Inventory data.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft released a report template, which is a great place to start to get data loaded and processed.\n...\n**The Gateway Performance Report template,** developed by BlueGranite, uses Microsoft’s as a starting point but expands and builds upon it to provide an actionable, easy-to-navigate, and use, report.\n...\nInstructions on how to set up Gateway Logging and the Microsoft Gateway Performance Power BI template download are available here.","date":"2020-02-11"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.\nThis is an amazing piece of work that is already being used by several very large customers.","date":"2024-10-27"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-data-gateways","title":"Power BI implementation planning: Data gateways - Microsoft Learn","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"There are three types of gateway.\n- On-premises data gateway (standard mode)\n- On-premises data gateway (personal mode), known as a *personal gateway*\n- Virtual network (VNet) gateway service\n...\n- **Planning**: Make key decisions as described earlier and define the technical specifications for a gateway machine, including initial system resources.\nThe gateway owner should also ensure that a support plan is in place.\n- **Installation**: Select an appropriate machine to install the gateway software and perform first-time installation and setup.\n- **Management**: Change gateway settings or preferences for optimization (such as configuring streaming instead of spooling data) or monitoring purposes (such as configuring performance logging).\nThe gateway owner also makes decisions about when to scale up (add more resources to the gateway machines) or scale out (install more gateways in the cluster).\n- **Testing**: Validate the gateway usage during first-time setup, ensuring that sufficient resources are available for the gateway machines.\nThe gateway owner should also test monthly updates before installing them.\n- **Updating**: Update and install the gateway software and supporting items (like connector software) on a timely basis.\n- **Monitoring**: Monitor gateway uptime and health, including the collection of the gateway log files that allow monitoring for issues and anomalous activity.\n...\n- Install Windows updates and perform other server maintenance.\n- Update the gateway software.\nThe gateway software is updated monthly, and Microsoft only supports the last six releases of the on-premises gateway.\n- Update custom data connectors, drivers, and libraries, when necessary.\n...\nYou can test gateways by first applying the update to gateways that support development and test environments.\n...\n- **Automate testing processes**: Use the Power BI REST APIs to test the data refresh for import data items and to evaluate DAX queries.\nEnsure that you can catch and log refresh failures or query errors.\n...\nConsider the following points about updating gateways.\n- In your centralized portal, document the current version of the gateway.\n- Apply updates during periods of historically low gateway usage.\n- Use a consistent a consistent schedule when you test and apply updates.\nIf monthly updates are too often, ensure that your gateways are updated at least quarterly.\n- Update one gateway cluster machine at a time.\nBy rotating through each machine, you can avoid downtime.\n...\n- **Create a process for installing updates**: Determine how often you'll install gateway software updates and the process to be followed.\nAim to update gateways within one to three months of the update release.\n- **Install gateway updates first on development and test**: Ensure that development and test gateways are updated prior to production and used for initial testing.","date":"2024-12-30"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"This presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n…","date":"2022-09-28"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"Power BI Dev Camp Session 28 -  How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"## Microsoft Power BI\n...\nIn this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\n...\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://azuremarketplace.microsoft.com/en-us/marketplace/apps/carloconsultingllc1611096483318.fabric_monitor_02?tab=overview","title":"Business Intelligence Monitoring Accelerator and Support","domain":"azuremarketplace.microsoft.com","summary":"# Business Intelligence Monitoring Accelerator and Support\n## Carlo Consulting LLC\n...\n## Full End to End Power BI and Microsoft Fabric Monitoring Deployment Solution and Support\nIntroducing a comprehensive Data Monitoring solution designed specifically for technical leaders who manage Power BI and Microsoft Fabric environments.\nThis accelerator is engineered to pull data from Microsoft Power BI APIs, Microsoft Fabric APIs, and Microsoft Graph APIs, creating a holistic view of activities and a detailed catalog of items across your entire Power BI and Microsoft Fabric Tenant.\n...\nGateway Monitoring: Monitor the health and performance of your data gateways to ensure seamless data flow and connectivity.\n...\nWe also provide technical support for the Fabric Unified Admin Monitoring (FUAM) project, an open-source initiative that provides extensive monitoring capabilities for Power BI and Fabric environments.","date":"2024-06-26"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-troubleshooting-solution-rui-romano","title":"A Power BI Gateway Monitoring & Troubleshooting Solution","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"So, to ease the pain of these #powerbi admins I created a simple open-source solution that require admins to publish a simple & lightweight #powershell script to each gateway server that incrementally send & classify (by gatewayid, log type, timestamp) the gateway logs to a central storage account allowing admins to:\nIn the end you can just explore the logs using Azure Portal or Azure Storage Explorer:\nOr using a built-in Power BI template:\nDownload solution & setup instructions here","date":"2022-07-15"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Nexacu BI Monitor gateway monitoring Power BI","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\n...\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\nThere are other values in the config file `C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config` that you can update as needed:\n- **ReportFilePath**: Determines the path where the four log files are stored.\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\nIf you use a service account for the gateway other than *PBIEgwService*, replace this part of the path with the service account name.\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n- **ReportFileSizeInBytes**: Determines the size of the file to maintain.\nThe default value is 104,857,600.\n- **QueryExecutionAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the query execution information is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\n- **SystemCounterAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the system counter is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nIf you're investigating slow-performing queries, in addition to using the performance monitoring feature, you can temporarily enable **Additional logging** to gather additional log information, including mashup engine logs, query strings, and verbose-level tracing.\nThese logs are written to the same place as your normal gateway logs.\nTo do this, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\nThe BLOB storage or the\n{ts:490} data lake will have a folder for each gateway and on each folder you have the logs.\n...\n{ts:529} easy access to the log, so you don't need as a power BI admin or a tenant t admin.\nYou don't need to give permissions to.\nTo the gateway servers remote desktop permissions\n{ts:539} to the Gateway service to get access to the logs and now you can just share a data Lake connection and an access to a folder or all the folders if you want.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\n…\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.\n...\nLet's imagine that\n{ts:1211} this scenario you go to each gateway y, you copy and copy the logs or export the logs using the.\n{ts:1219} The gateway application and you should end up with something like this so in here.\nI have a gateways from gateway logs from\n{ts:1229} 2 gateways so this was an export from the gateway so o I can copy this folder.\nAnd I can open the front desk\n{ts:1238} templates, so not the from the from this template I think I already have it here and you will see a parameter window like this you need\n{ts:1249} d to specify the data location, in this case it's not the default location of the gateway, so let's s put the folder here, the folder that","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"challenge faced by Power BI Admins is how-to monitor & troubleshoot multiple on-premises Data Gateway Clusters.\nWith each cluster having multiple servers that are responsible for connecting their data sources to the Power BI service in the cloud, investigating errors or performance issues across the gateway logs can become a complex task.\nThis presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n…","date":"2022-09-28"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Power-BI-Community-Blog/Power-BI-Gateway-Monitoring-amp-Administrating-Part-1/ba-p/750509","title":"Power BI: Gateway Monitoring & Administrating- Part 1","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"# PowerBI Service Account\nIn order to begin the first most step is to have a Service Account with a Pro License, here we are going to call some API's.\nSo instead of using your persona account, you should have a service account with a pro license.\nOnce you have a service account you should be add these service account to all your Gateway's as a Administrator.\n...\nWe are going to automating the part of generating the bearer token to call the API.\nSo for this the best way is to use PowerShell, we can also use PBI M-query to generate the Bearer but in that case we need to provide the service account user name and password with in the report which is a kind of unsecure where we expose the username and password with-in the report.\n...\n```\n-executionpolicy bypass -command \"& {.\nC:\\Jay\\works\\powershell\\PowerBI.ps1; Get-AADToken -Username \"xxxx@xx.com\" -Password \"xxxyyyzzz\" -ClientId \"xxxx11-xx11-x1-x12-xxxx\" -path \"C:\\Jay\\works\\pbi\\Gateway_Report\" -fileName \"Bearer.txt\"} -WindowStyle Hidden\n```\nIn this command you will give the\n1. username\n2. password\n3. clientid\n4. Path where the Bearer token file should be generated\n5. FileName for the Bearer token file\nNow we are all set to create our report in PowerBI\n# Creating Gateway Monitoring Report in PBI Desktop\nThis is the most interesting and important part, where we are going to call the API directly in our PBI Report.\nLet's gets started with a blank query.\nOur most of the part will be in the Advanced Editor where we will define our custom **M-Queries**\n...\nThis API will give you the Main Cluster gateway and it's corresponding clusters gateways\nNow lets continue our M-query editor in the PBI Desktop, before writing the code let's put up the steps needed to achieve our goal\n1. We need to call the Gateway API\n2. In order to call the API we need the Bearer Token which we already have it in a Text File called (Bearer.txt) from the previous powershell script through automation from Task Schedular\nThe Code for this is\n```\nlet\n**TokenType = \"Bearer\",\nToken = Text.FromBinary(File.Contents(\"C:\\Jay\\works\\pbi\\Gateway_Report\\Bearer.txt\")),\nSource = Json.Document(Web.Contents(\"https://api.powerbi.com/v2.0/myorg/me/gatewayclusters?$expand=memberGateways&$skip=0\",[Headers=[Authorization=TokenType & \" \" & Token]])),**\nvalue = Source[value],\n#\"Converted to Table\" = Table.FromList(value, Splitter.SplitByNothing(), null, null, ExtraValues.Error),\n#\"Expanded Column1\" = Table.ExpandRecordColumn(#\"Converted to Table\", \"Column1\", {\"id\", \"name\", \"dataSourceIds\", \"type\", \"options\", \"memberGateways\"}, {\"id\", \"name\", \"dataSourceIds\", \"type\", \"options\", \"memberGateways\"}),\n#\"Expanded options\" = Table.ExpandRecordColumn(#\"Expanded Column1\", \"options\", {\"CloudDatasourceRefresh\", \"CustomConnectors\"}, {\"CloudDatasourceRefresh\", \"CustomConnectors\"})\n...\nTricks If you looked into PowerBI Gateways API documentation it is wrongly mentioned that this API will give the status, but actually it will not give you the status of the gateway.\nI've confirmed this from officially from the PowerBI community forum\nInorder to get the current gateway status we need to call another api called Gateways - Get Gateway unforunately this API call we need to for each and every gateways seperately and not as a bulk operation.\nLet's continue our coding, the below code will call the Get Gateway API for each and every gateway id which we obtained from the previous result\n```\n#\"Add Column\" = Table.AddColumn(#\"Expanded options\", \"DataFromURLsColumn\", each Json.Document(Web.Contents(\"https://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/\",[RelativePath= \"gateways/\" & [id] , Headers=[Authorization=TokenType & \" \" & Token]])))\n```\nNow finally our code looks like the below one\n```\nlet\nTokenType = \"Bearer\",\n...\nTips : In order to identify the gateway environment like Production/non-production, you can specify in the **gatewayDepartment ** while configuring the gateway itself, which will be used for us to create filters in our report.","date":"2019-08-07"},{"url":"https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","title":"GitHub - RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","domain":"github.com","summary":"This project aim to help organizations with multiple gateway clusters centralize all their gateway logs and reports into a central storage (ADLS Gen 2) and allow easy and quick exploration of those logs either by:\n- Easily access all the gateway logs without having to remote access to the gateway server\n- Explore the logs with a Power BI Report\n- Explore the logs using a SPARK Engine like Azure Synapse Analytics\n**To quickly analyze the logs of gateways you dont need to run/deploy the powershell scripts** its possible to use the disk Power BI template and directly point to the exported gateway logs.\nThe powershell & central storage is only required if you want to keep an fully automated gateway monitoring solution with history log data retention.\n...\n- Azure Data Lake Storage Account (ADLS Gen 2) with Hierarchical Namespace enabled\n- PowerShell 7 on the Gateway Server, with the following modules installed: Az.Accounts, Az.Storage\n...\n# Power BI Template\nThere are two Power BI templates available:\n- Lake Template - When gateway log data resides in the ADLS Gen 2 lake\n- Disk Template - When gateway log data reside in disk, useful to analyze the logs directly from the gateway server or gateway log export.\nYou dont need to deploy the powershell scripts to use this template, only need to have access to gateway logs.\n## Template Parameters\nAfter opening the Power BI Template file (.pbit) the following parameter window will popup:\n|Parameter|Description|\n|--|--|\n|DataLocation|URL Path to the root folder including the container name (default: '/pbigatewaymonitor/RAW'), using the Distributed File System (DFS) endpoint of the storage account, ex: https://storage.**dfs**.core.windows.net/**pbigatewaymonitor/raw**; Or the path to the disk location with logs if the disk template is used|\n|NumberDays|Filter to the log files to be fetched, if '10' Power BI will read only the latest 10 days of logs/queries/counters Default: null (all days)|\n|SinceDate|Only read log/query files since the specified date.\nThis parameter overrides 'NumberDays' Default: null (all days)|\n|MaxLogTextLength|Max size of text column of logs.\nDefault: 1000|\n|LogFilters|Comma separated file names of log files to be fetched.\nDefault: \"gatewayerrors,gatewayinfo\" If 'None' log files will be excluded|\n|GatewayFilters|Comma separated gateway id's.\nDefault: All Gateways|","date":"2022-02-19"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","summary":"I've been using the gateway and other performance monitor counters via PBI Desktop on a project recently.","date":"2019-06-17"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-troubleshooting-solution-rui-romano","title":"A Power BI Gateway Monitoring & Troubleshooting Solution - LinkedIn","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"So, to ease the pain of these #powerbi admins I created a simple open-source solution that require admins to publish a simple & lightweight #powershell script to each gateway server that incrementally send & classify (by gatewayid, log type, timestamp) the gateway logs to a central storage account allowing admins to:\nIn the end you can just explore the logs using Azure Portal or Azure Storage Explorer:\nOr using a built-in Power BI template:\nDownload solution & setup instructions here","date":"2022-06-26"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/sadik-sheikh-095924219_monitor-gateway-logs-in-power-bi-report-activity-7337250750328143875-_udW","title":"📊 Monitor Gateway Logs in Power BI Report | Sadik Sheikh","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"📊 Monitor Gateway Logs in Power BI Report 1.\nEnable Gateway Performance Logs In Gateway Settings, enable Performance Monitoring.\nLogs are stored in: C:\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\PerformanceLogs 2.\nCreate a Power BI Report Load .csv or .txt log files into Power BI Desktop.\nUse columns like: Timestamp ActivityType Duration Status ErrorCode 3.\nBuild Visuals Line Chart: Refresh duration over time.\nBar Chart: Errors by type.\nTable: Detailed log entries with filters.\n4. Schedule Refresh Publish the report to Power BI Service.\nSchedule refresh to monitor logs regularly.\nMicrosoft Power BI","date":"2025-06-07"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI monitoring tool gateway health check community GitHub","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://maqhatimd.github.io/case-studies/health-check","title":"Automatically Check Power BI Reports for Critical Errors","domain":"maqhatimd.github.io","summary":"We created an automated solution that reviews Power BI reports and automatically notifies the developers if the tool finds any issues.\nWe created a browser-based utility that opens Power BI reports, checks the load times of each component, takes a screenshot of each page, and emails a diagnostic report.\nSetup of the Power BI health check solution requires the user to link all Power BI reports that need monitoring.\nOnce the reports are linked, the solution automatically checks the reports and provides regular notifications.\nThe solution is cross-OS compatible.\nOur Power BI health check solution starts by creating an HTTP request for each command.\nThe HTTP server then determines the steps to be performed by controlling web page elements using XPaths.\nThe gateway status is timed via a built-in stopwatch to determine the browser load time from the data server.\nFinally, the load time report and screenshots are sent to recipients via an SMTP server.\nOur Power BI health check solution notifies developers of report failures earlier than previous methods, allowing for quicker resolution of errors.\nMarketing teams can now concentrate on sales and revenue figures without distractions from Power BI performance issues.","date":"2017-08-31"},{"url":"https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","title":"RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"RuiRomano / **\npbigtwmonitor ** Public\n# RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nThis project aim to help organizations with multiple gateway clusters centralize all their gateway logs and reports into a central storage (ADLS Gen 2) and allow easy and quick exploration of those logs either by:\n- Easily access all the gateway logs without having to remote access to the gateway server\n- Explore the logs with a Power BI Report\n- Explore the logs using a SPARK Engine like Azure Synapse Analytics\n**To quickly analyze the logs of gateways you dont need to run/deploy the powershell scripts** its possible to use the disk Power BI template and directly point to the exported gateway logs.\nThe powershell & central storage is only required if you want to keep an fully automated gateway monitoring solution with history log data retention.\n...\nThere are two Power BI templates available:\n- Lake Template - When gateway log data resides in the ADLS Gen 2 lake\n- Disk Template - When gateway log data reside in disk, useful to analyze the logs directly from the gateway server or gateway log export.\nYou dont need to deploy the powershell scripts to use this template, only need to have access to gateway logs.","date":"2025-12-10"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance.md","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"|Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance|This article provides ways for you to monitor and optimize the performance of the on-premises data gateway activities.|conceptual|05/06/2024|\n...\nTo monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\n...\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n:::image type=\"content\" source=\"media/service-gateway-performance/gateway-folder-path-pop-up.png\" alt-text=\"Pop-up for the folder path.\":::\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nTo do this, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n...\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/10/16/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-and-more-using-microsoft-fabric/","title":"Power BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"This article describes a solution built with Microsoft Fabric.\n...\nIf you want to track the well-being of your on-premises data gateways but do not want to build something, I recommend using the solution by Rui Romano you can find here: https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nI built this monitoring solution focusing on the well-being of the on-premises data gateway.\n...\nFor tracking the well-being of the on-premises data gateway(s), I use the REST API endpoint\n`gateways` described here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/power-bi/gateways/get-gateways.\n...\nThe following image shows how you add the Service Principal (not the security group, this is only for demonstration purposes) as an Admin to the on-premises data gateway(s) you want to monitor, the second resource that requires authorization next to the REST API.\n...\nHere, you will find the notebook “tenantMonitoring_gateways.ipynb” (https://tommartens-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/tom_minceddata_com/EaxaEdtqMo5FvUdSstAAuoEBmo2PUYj4kXdqEqLCjoFUhA?e=MrF5eU).\n...\nBasically, the current solution is simple: I have a Fabric-enabled workspace, a lakehouse, and a notebook, but the solution will grow during the next iterations.\n...\nInside the lakehouse, I created a subfolder “GatewayInformation,” inside the files section.\nThis is the place where I store the result of a REST API endpoint request as a CSV file, of course, after some data transformations."},{"url":"https://github.com/kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring","title":"kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring: Templates for monitoring Power BI ...","domain":"github.com","summary":"kerski / **\npbi-dataops-monitoring ** Public\n...\nTemplates for monitoring Power BI components and related Power Platform elements for your data analytics projects.\n...\nEmbracing the DataOps principle \"Monitor Quality and Performance\", this repository provides a set of templates and instructions for monitoring your Power BI solution/projects.\n...\nThe Power BI templates in this repository help you monitor for issues such as:\n### Power BI Issues\n1. Unresolved Failed Dataset Refreshes - If the latest dataset refresh has failed this template identifies the issue.\n2. Unscheduled Dataset Refresh - If a dataset fails multiple times then the schedule refresh is disabled.\nThis can lead to latent data and this template highlights this issue.\n3. Long-running dataset refreshs - If a dataset is taking longer than two hours to run this is generally a concern and should be investigated.\n4. Unresolved Failed Dataflows Refreshes - If the latest dataflow has failed this template identifies the issue.\n5. Number of Data Quality Issue Tables within Dataset - If the dataset has hidden tables that start with \"Errors in\", this template identifies the issue.\nThis indicates existing or prior cell-level errors that need to be resolved (and tables deleted).\n- *Note*: This check does not work as of January 2025.\nMicrosoft locked down the ability for the ExecuteQueries endpoint to access Dynamic Management Views and INFO functions.\nINFO functions should be restored at some point, but there is no publicly available timeframe.\n### Power Automate Issues\n1. Unresolved Failure with Scheduled Power Automate Flows - If the latest schedule flow in Power Automate fails this template identifies the issue.\n2. Failed Manually Triggered Power Automate Flows in past 72 hours - If manually triggered flows (often through Power Automate visuals) fail this template highlights this issue.\n...\nTo help automate checks of the issues, the templates use two open-source customer connectors.\n...\nPower Query Custom Data Connector for Power BI REST APIs\nPower Query Custom Data Connector for Power Automate REST APIs\n#### On-Premises Gateway\nThe custom data connectors will need to be installed in the a Power BI Gateway in order to refresh the monitoring template in the Power BI Service.\nFor more information on installing a custom data connector with a gateway please see: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-gateway-custom-connectors.\n## Installation\n1. Download the appropriate .pbit file from the latest Release section.\n2. Open the .pbit template.\n3. You will see a prompt as shown in the Figure 1\n*Figure 1 - Example of Template Prompt*\n1. In the field \"Workspace(s) Query\" enter the OData query that identifies the workspace or workspaces you wish to monitor.\nFor example if the workspace you wish to monitor is named 'Test1' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1'\".\nFor another example if the workspaces you wish to monitor are named 'Test1' and 'Test2' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1' or name eq 'Test2'\".\n1. Select the \"Load\" button.\nIf this is your first time using the custom data connectors you will be prompted to sign into Office 365.\nPlease follow the instructions to sign in and then choose the \"Connect\" button.\n*Figure 2 - Sign In Prompt*\n1. and the template will load.\n*Figure 3 - Example of Refresh In Progress* 6.\nWhen completed you should see the Home tab of the monitoring report.\n*Figure 4 - Example of Power BI Monitoring Report*\n1. Please save this file.\nIf you do not have a gateway installed, you will need to refresh this file locally to pull the latest information.\n...\nSince the connector uses the OAuth of the logged in accountto get the Power Automate flows, it's possible that you do not want to monitor all the Power Automate flows that are retrieved by the template.\nTo specify the Power Automate flows you wish to monitor, within the template follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Power Automate Flows\".\n3. Filter the \"Name\" and specify with flows you wish to monitor.\n*Figure 5 - Screenshot of where to filter the Power Automate flows that you wish to monitor.*\n...\nIf there are datasets that do not require an automated refresh scheduled with the Power BI Dataset settings, you can add those datasets to an exception list.\nWithin the template, please follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Refresh Exception List\"\n3. Within the \"Query Settings\" pane, click the cog icon next to the \"Source\" step.\n*Figure 6 - Screenshot of where to add/update datasets on the refresh exception list.*\n1. Add/Update the names of the datasets you wish to have an exception.\nWhen complete, click the \"OK\" button.\nPlease note the name of the dataset must match **exactly** the name in the Power BI service.","date":"2023-03-24"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-platform/API-to-monitor-On-premises-Data-Gateway-SQL-Server-connection/m-p/4911489","title":"Re: API to monitor On-premises Data Gateway / SQL Server ...","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"Your requirement is valid, but not possible today because Microsoft has not exposed a direct real-time Gateway Connection Status API.\n...\nYou can monitor your on-premises data gateway and its SQL Server connections using supported REST APIs.\n**Check Gateway Status (Online/Offline)**\n*GET https://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/gateways/{gatewayId}*\nLook for the JSON field gatewayStatus (e.g., \"Live\" or \"Offline\").\nThis returns the cluster-level connectivity status.\n**Check Individual Data Source Connection Status**\n*GET https://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/gateways/{gatewayId}/datasources/{datasourceId}/status*\nIt returns a 200 OK if reachable, or a JSON error (e.g., DM_GWPipeline_Client_GatewayUnreachable) on failure.\n...\nHi @CharlieFab , I'm going to answer directly your question...\nI'm going to assume you know how to interact with the Fabric/PowerBI API's via Postman, that is getting the token and calling interfaces, if you do not know how to do this, I recommend you to check my article Removing Orphan Fabric Deployment Pipelines and from the Solution, do Step 1: Getting the Access Token and Step 2.\nConfigure Postman Authorization.\nOnce ready, you need to get the Gateway Id via API Gateways - List Gateways - REST API (Core) | Microsoft Learn or the Fabric portal as shown below\nAnd once you locate the gateway Id,  you need PowerBI API interface Gateways - Get Gateways - REST API (Power BI Power BI REST APIs) | Microsoft Learn\nExample\n```\nhttps://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/gateways/cbe58db1-b37e-4951-8bdf-28de8a8d2508\n```\nResult  \"gatewayStatus\": \"Live\"\nAnd there you have it, exactly what you need!\nThe status of the gateway in plain site, just get the JSON response and get gatewayStatus member 😁🤞 ...\nI guess this is the answer for your question so, please mark as solution and a kudos would be nice ...\nbest of lucks.\nBTW.\nAdditional note for all the people out there, there's currently two API's that work on Fabric:\n#1.\nThe Fabric API and\n```\nhttps://api.fabric.microsoft.com/v1/\n```\n#2.\nthe PowerBI API\n```\nhttps://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg\n```\nI guesse eventually everything will be migrated to Fabric API, but until then, is worth to check on both APIs 🤞","date":"2026-01-05"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"This presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n### Transcript","date":"2022-09-28"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features/gateway-monitoring","title":"Power BI Gateway Monitoring | SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"# Spot personal gateways and connectivity risks before they cause failures.\n- Find every personal gateway hiding in your tenant\n- Know the instant a gateway goes offline\n- See which datasets break when a gateway fails\n...\nTrack online/offline status and alert when gateways go down.\n...\nConnect via Cloud Connect or the optional agent — SummitView enumerates all gateways and data sources automatically.\n2\nGateway health, version, and type are monitored continuously.\n3\nPersonal gateways and offline gateways trigger alerts with affected-dataset context.\n...\nGateway Monitoring works with all Power BI license types.\nIt monitors gateways at the tenant level — both enterprise gateways managed by IT and personal gateways installed by individual users.\n...\nA single health score for your Power BI tenant, updated daily with trends and actionable recommendations.","date":"2026-02-01"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"PowerShell on-premises data gateway health check script GitHub","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-powershell-support.md","title":"PowerShell support for on-premises data gateway clusters - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"- Retrieve the list of gateway clusters available for a user.\n- Retrieve the list of gateway instances registered in a cluster and their online or offline status.\n- Modify the enable or disable status for a gateway instance within a cluster and other gateway properties.\n...\nTo install these cmdlets, run the following command in an elevated PowerShell session:\n```\nInstall-Module -Name OnPremisesDataGatewayHAMgmt\n```","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-11-17-on-premises-data-gateway-configuration-management/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Configuration and Management","domain":"www.devladlog.com","summary":"### Automated Health Check Script\n```\n## Gateway health monitoring script (run via scheduled task every 5 minutes)\nfunction Test-GatewayHealth {\n```powershell\nparam(\n[string]$EmailTo = \"ops@contoso.com\",\n[string]$SmtpServer = \"smtp.contoso.com\"\n$report = @{\nTimestamp = Get-Date\nServerName = $env:COMPUTERNAME\nChecks = @()\nOverallStatus = \"Healthy\"\n## Check 1: Gateway service running\n$service = Get-Service -Name \"PBIEgwService\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n$serviceCheck = @{\nName = \"Gateway Service\"\nStatus = if ($service -and $service.Status -eq \"Running\") { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"❌ Fail\" }\nDetails = \"Status: $($service.Status)\"\n$report.Checks += $serviceCheck\nif ($serviceCheck.Status -like \"*Fail*\") {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Unhealthy\"\n## Check 2: CPU usage\n$cpu = (Get-Counter \"\\Processor(_Total)\\% Processor Time\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$cpuCheck = @{\nName = \"CPU Usage\"\nStatus = if ($cpu -lt 80) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($cpu, 1))%\"\n$report.Checks += $cpuCheck\nif ($cpu -gt 90) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 3: Available memory\n$memAvailMB = (Get-Counter \"\\Memory\\Available MBytes\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$memCheck = @{\nName = \"Available Memory\"\nStatus = if ($memAvailMB -gt 2048) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($memAvailMB, 0)) MB\"\n$report.Checks += $memCheck\nif ($memAvailMB -lt 1024) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 4: Gateway log errors (last hour)\n$logPath = \"C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Gateway*.log\"\n$logs = Get-ChildItem $logPath | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1\nif ($logs) {\n$recentErrors = Get-Content $logs.FullName -Tail 1000 |\nWhere-Object { $_ -match \"ERROR|FATAL\" } |\nWhere-Object { (Get-Date) - [datetime]::Parse(($_ -split '\\|')[0]) -lt (New-TimeSpan -Hours 1) }\n$errorCheck = @{\nName = \"Recent Errors\"\nStatus = if ($recentErrors.Count -eq 0) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$($recentErrors.Count) errors in last hour\"\n}\n$report.Checks += $errorCheck\nif ($recentErrors.Count -gt 10) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n}\n## Check 5: Disk space\n$disk = Get-WmiObject Win32_LogicalDisk -Filter \"DeviceID='C:'\"\n$freeSpaceGB = [math]::Round($disk.FreeSpace / 1GB, 2)\n$diskCheck = @{\nName = \"Disk Space (C:)\"\nStatus = if ($freeSpaceGB -gt 10) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$freeSpaceGB GB free\"\n$report.Checks += $diskCheck\nif ($freeSpaceGB -lt 5) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Generate report\n$emailBody = @\"```\n<html>\n<head><style>\ntable { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }\nth, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px; text-align: left; }\n...\n.pass { color: green; }\n.warning { color: orange; }\n.fail { color: red; }\n</style></head>\n<body>\n<h2>Gateway Health Report - $($report.ServerName)</h2>\n...\nforeach ($check in $report.Checks) {\n...\n$emailBody += @\"```\n</table>\n</body>\n</html>\n\"@\n```text\n## Send email if unhealthy or warning\nif ($report.OverallStatus -ne \"Healthy\") {\n$subject = \"⚠️ Gateway Health Alert - $($report.ServerName) - $($report.OverallStatus)\"\nSend-MailMessage -To $EmailTo -From \"gateway-monitor@contoso.com\" -Subject $subject `\n-Body $emailBody -BodyAsHtml -SmtpServer $SmtpServer\nWrite-Host \"Alert sent: $subject\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway health check passed\" -ForegroundColor Green\nreturn $report```\n## Run health check\nTest-GatewayHealth\n```\n...\n## Script to check for and install gateway updates\nfunction Update-Gateway {\n```powershell\nparam(\n[switch]$AutoRestart\nWrite-Host \"Checking for gateway updates...\"\n-ForegroundColor Cyan\n## Get current gateway version\n$gatewayExe = \"C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\EnterpriseGatewayConfigurator.exe\"\nif (-not (Test-Path $gatewayExe)) {\nWrite-Host \"❌ Gateway not found\" -ForegroundColor Red\nreturn\n$currentVersion = (Get-Item $gatewayExe).VersionInfo.FileVersion\nWrite-Host \"Current version: $currentVersion\" -ForegroundColor White\n## Download latest installer\n$downloadUrl = \"https://download.microsoft.com/download/D/A/1/DA1FDDB8-6DA8-4F50-B4D0-18019591E182/GatewayInstall.exe\"\n$installerPath = \"$env:TEMP\\GatewayInstall_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').exe\"\nWrite-Host \"Downloading latest installer...\"\n-ForegroundColor Cyan\nInvoke-WebRequest -Uri $downloadUrl -OutFile $installerPath\n$newVersion = (Get-Item $installerPath).VersionInfo.FileVersion\nWrite-Host \"Latest version: $newVersion\" -ForegroundColor White\nif ($currentVersion -eq $newVersion) {\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway is up to date\" -ForegroundColor Green\nreturn\nWrite-Host \"Update available: $currentVersion → $newVersion\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n## Create pre-update backup\n$backupPath = \"C:\\Gateway-Backups\\Pre-Update-$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')\"\nNew-Item -Path $backupPath -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null\n## Backup config files\nCopy-Item -Path \"C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\*.config\" -Destination $backupPath -Force\nWrite-Host \"Config backed up to: $backupPath\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n## Install update\nWrite-Host \"Installing gateway update...\"\n-ForegroundColor Cyan\n$logPath = \"C:\\Logs\\GatewayUpdate-$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss').log\"\nStart-Process -FilePath $installerPath -ArgumentList \"-quiet\", \"-norestart\", \"-log\", $logPath -Wait -NoNewWindow\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway updated successfully\" -ForegroundColor Green\nWrite-Host \"Log: $logPath\"\nif ($AutoRestart) {\nWrite-Host \"Restarting gateway service...\"\n-ForegroundColor Yellow\n...\n}\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"⚠️ Manual restart required\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n}```\n## Run update (with auto-restart)\nUpdate-Gateway -AutoRestart\n```\n...\n4. **Implement automated monitoring** with health check scripts","date":"2025-11-17"},{"url":"https://github.com/jeevan-vj/iamjeevan/blob/master/data/blog/health-check-implementation-in-powershell.md","title":"iamjeevan/data/blog/health-check-implementation-in-powershell.md at master · jeevan-vj/iamjeevan","domain":"github.com","summary":"Here is simple Powershell function to check health check.\nSolution\n```\n$url = \"https://api.healthcheck\" # Replace with your desired URL\n$maxRetryCount = 3 # Maximum number of retries\n$retryDelaySec = 5 # Delay between retries in seconds\nfunction Check-UrlHealth {\nparam(\n[string]$Url\n)\n$retryCount = 0\n$success = $false\ndo {\ntry {\n$response = (Invoke-WebRequest -Uri $url -UseBasicParsing)\n$statusCode = $response.StatusCode\nWrite-Host \"Endpoint returned status '$statusCode'\"\nif ($statusCode -eq 200) {\n$success = $true\nWrite-Host \"Health check successful for $Url\"\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"Health check failed for $Url.\nStatus code: $statusCode\"\n}\n} catch {\nWrite-Host \"An error occurred during health check for $Url\"\nWrite-Host $_.Exception.Message\nif ($retryCount -lt $maxRetryCount) {\nWrite-Host \"Retrying in $retryDelaySec seconds...\"\nStart-Sleep -Seconds $retryDelaySec\n}\n}\n}\nwhile (($success -ne $TRUE) -and (++$retryCount -lt $maxRetryCount))\nif (-not $success) {\nWrite-Host \"Health check failed after $maxRetryCount attempts.\"\n}\n}\n# Call the function to check the URL health\nCheck-UrlHealth -Url $url\n```\n:)","date":"2021-08-20"},{"url":"https://github.com/topics/health-check?l=powershell&o=desc&s=forks","title":"Build software better, together","domain":"github.com","summary":"Active Directory's Health Check script that generates a full HTML report of the environment's health, security and status based on Microsoft's Best Practices.\n...\nPowershell module to check AD health\nDEPRECATED 2023-09-08 - A GitHub Action that pings a given url and prints the status and content of the response.\nThis PowerShell script performs a comprehensive health check on both the domain and domain controllers, ensuring the overall stability and integrity of the network infrastructure.\nIt examines various critical aspects such as connectivity, replication, and service status to provide a comprehensive overview of the system's health.","date":"2024-08-19"},{"url":"https://github.com/joeshen117/connectionCheckerScript","title":"GitHub - joeshen117/connectionCheckerScript: A PowerShell script you can run to check all On-premises Data Gateways used by API connections","domain":"github.com","summary":"joeshen117 / **\nconnectionCheckerScript ** Public\n...\n**This repository contains a PowerShell script you can run to list all On-premises Data Gateways used by Logic App API connections and save to a CSV file.** For more information on finding correlations between Logic App API connections and On-premises Data Gateways, please visit my blog here.\n...\nFind out which On-premises Data Gateways are used by which API connection resources.\n...\nA PowerShell script you can run to check all On-premises Data Gateways used by API connections","date":"2021-04-14"},{"url":"https://github.com/fleschutz/PowerShell/blob/main/scripts/check-health.ps1","title":"PowerShell/scripts/check-health.ps1 at main · fleschutz/PowerShell","domain":"github.com","summary":"Checks the system health\n.DESCRIPTION\nThis PowerShell script queries the system health of the local computer (hardware, software, and network) and prints it.\n...\n.LINK\nhttps://github.com/fleschutz/PowerShell\n.NOTES\nAuthor: Markus Fleschutz | License: CC0","date":"2020-04-30"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-onprem-faq.yml","title":"service-gateway-onprem-faq.yml - GitHub","domain":"github.com","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=963KPiL2PZk","title":"055 - On Premises Data Gateway Monitoring Status - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this video, we are going to discuss how we can monitor the status of our On-premises Data Gateways through the use of Power BI Powershell CMDlets.\nWe will orchestrate these Powershell calls by using Power Automate Desktop.\nLinks from Video:\nDownload Powershell 7: https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/releases/tag/v7.0.3\nGateway Documentation: https://powerbi.microsoft.com/en-us/blog/on-premises-data-gateway-management-via-powershell-public-preview/\nPowerShell Scripts: https://middlewareinthecloud.com/2020/10/25/on-premises-data-gateway-monitoring-using-powershell/\n...\n{ts:125} that just to help save you some time if you're trying to set this up for yourself so number one\n{ts:129} you do need to download powershell 7 and powershell 7 is a prerequisite because\n{ts:136} of the power bi powershell command lines that we're going to use those have been written in powershell 7\n{ts:144} and as a result we need to make sure this is installed on our machine so here's where you can go ahead and\n{ts:150} grab the latest version of powershell and i will include a link in the description of this video\n{ts:157} to help you out from that perspective the other thing we're going to need is an azure ad\n{ts:165} app so when we go ahead and we run this process we are going to use service principle\n{ts:171} and we will need to go create that azure ad app in order to support\n{ts:175} that service principle requirement now when you go ahead and set this up the most important thing to do here\n{ts:181} is you've got api permissions and you need the power bi service and then these are the two operations\n{ts:188} that we're going to be calling underneath the hood tenant.read.all and tenantreadwrite.all\n{ts:195} now do note these do require admin consent and for good reason when we run this we are going to run\n{ts:201} this using an organization scope so we are going to go ahead and discover\n{ts:206} all gateways in our environment that is certainly a requirement for all of this monitoring scenario\n...\n{ts:219} actual powershell scripts that you do write but we are going to take advantage of\n{ts:224} this data gateway module now this is provided by microsoft and so\n{ts:229} that is what we need to do is we need to go ahead and install it because shortly here we're going to go\n{ts:234} ahead and import that as a part of our script and naturally it needs to be there in\n{ts:239} order for us to go ahead and use it once again more information at the following link i'll include that in the\n{ts:244} description itself once it's installed then we can go ahead and we can\n{ts:249} import it and this will be part of our overall scripts that we'll talk a little bit\n...\n{ts:268} inside of powershell now we're going to talk about two specifically the get data gateway\n{ts:274} cluster and the get data gateway cluster status and so let's talk a little bit about\n...\n{ts:311} can essentially discover all of the gateways that exist in our environment\n{ts:316} by using the get data gateway cluster command now this won't give us the status we have to use the\n{ts:324} id value that is returned from this call in order to actually get the status of a specific\n...\n{ts:350} and then here is the subsequent call that i just spoke about so get data gateway cluster\n{ts:355} status and as part of that we do need to provide that scope which in this case is\n{ts:359} that organization you can also have scope for you know an individual user\n{ts:364} as well but as i mentioned we want to focus across the enterprise and then what we do need to do is feed\n{ts:369} in a gateway cluster id which was the same id that we talked about in the previous\n{ts:376} slide itself now when we make this call we will see a status in this case it'll be none which essentially means offline\n{ts:383} and then if a cluster is online we will see a value of live now in order to simplify\n...\n{ts:396} i've made those scripts available and what you can do is you can find them on my blog once\n...\n{ts:542} are returned and then one by one check their individual status and then we'll be\n...\n{ts:652} the next thing we're going to do is we're going to go ahead and run our powershell script\n{ts:656} now this is one of the scripts that's available on my blog post and if we look at it we'll find it here\n{ts:663} it's going to be getgatewaylist.ps1 let's go ahead and edit it and now as part of this what we're going to do is\n{ts:671} pass in argument so basically our app id our secret and our tenant we're then going to construct a secret\n{ts:676} string because we naturally need to do that and we will go ahead and we will\n{ts:682} connect to the number one we'll connect we'll create a connection to the gateway service account using\n{ts:689} those values then what we'll do is we'll import the data gateway\n{ts:694} module and then we will go ahead and execute the get data gateway cluster operation and we're going to do this at\n{ts:701} the organization scope and we're going to output the all of that data to a file itself\n...\n{ts:880} for our gateway then what we're going to go ahead and do is call our second script which is going to be get the\n{ts:886} gateway list status and we're going to provide the value basically that value\n{ts:891} of of this id and we need to pass that into the secondary script and so when we do that\n...\n{ts:964} is we're going to then get the status for that specific gateway because we've run\n{ts:970} the powershell script here we're going to read the file and in this case that's that\n{ts:976} currentgatewaystatus.txt and here this is where we now need to parse this value\n{ts:984} and so once again we're going to follow a fairly similar pattern where we will read it we will then go\n...\n{ts:1006} get me the first 14 characters now first 14 characters equal cluster status space then let's go\n{ts:1013} split it again and we'll once again use a delimiter and then what we'll do is we will write\n{ts:1019} the value of the cluster name also into that excel spreadsheet uh sorry the cluster status into that\n{ts:1026} excel spreadsheet in this case it's column c now the last step in our loop um is we're then going to go ahead and\n{ts:1033} get the gateway name and we will go ahead and capture that from like the original uh text because\n{ts:1039} we've already processed the id and we have also got the subtext just in case\n{ts:1045} it is a name and so that's why we're going to then just go ahead and process name once again if we see that the name","date":"2020-10-25"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI gateway log parser PowerShell open source","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","title":"RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"RuiRomano / **\npbigtwmonitor ** Public\n...\nThis project aim to help organizations with multiple gateway clusters centralize all their gateway logs and reports into a central storage (ADLS Gen 2) and allow easy and quick exploration of those logs either by:\n- Easily access all the gateway logs without having to remote access to the gateway server\n- Explore the logs with a Power BI Report\n- Explore the logs using a SPARK Engine like Azure Synapse Analytics\n**To quickly analyze the logs of gateways you dont need to run/deploy the powershell scripts** its possible to use the disk Power BI template and directly point to the exported gateway logs.\nThe powershell & central storage is only required if you want to keep an fully automated gateway monitoring solution with history log data retention.\n...\n- Azure Data Lake Storage Account (ADLS Gen 2) with Hierarchical Namespace enabled\n- PowerShell 7 on the Gateway Server, with the following modules installed: Az.Accounts, Az.Storage\n...\n### PowerShell Modules\nOn the gateway server ensure PowerShell 7 is installed and install the following required modules:\n- Az.Accounts\n- Az.Storage\nTo install the modules above, open a PowerShell 7 prompt and run the following commands:\n```\nInstall-Module Az.Accounts -MinimumVersion \"2.8.0\" -verbose\nInstall-Module Az.Storage -MinimumVersion \"4.6.0\" -verbose\nInstall-Module MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt -MinimumVersion \"1.2.1077\" -verbose\n```\n## Deploy scripts to Gateway Server\nOn each Gateway Server you should clone/copy this repo powershell scripts into a local folder (ex: c:\\PBIGTWMonitor)\n...\nOpen the Configuration file and configure the following settings:\n- StorageAccountConnStr\nOpen the ADLS Gen 2 storage account, go to \"Access Keys\" tab and copy the \"Connection String\" field:\n...\n# Power BI Template\nThere are two Power BI templates available:\n- Lake Template - When gateway log data resides in the ADLS Gen 2 lake\n- Disk Template - When gateway log data reside in disk, useful to analyze the logs directly from the gateway server or gateway log export.\nYou dont need to deploy the powershell scripts to use this template, only need to have access to gateway logs.","date":"2025-12-10"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\nHow\n...\nThe BLOB storage or the\n{ts:490} data lake will have a folder for each gateway and on each folder you have the logs.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\nYou probably can't leave it by default but if you change the location where the log files\n{ts:776} get stored you we also need to change the location, but by default if you don't do anything it's going to be stored in this location.\nAnd also.\n{ts:787} That's another thing that is very important is these two There are two part of the templates that you can use that is 1 called from disk and one from data\n...\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.\nIn the end, if we have time I will walk ou through on how to deploy it, but.\n{ts:857} Is that simple has creating a storage account or reusing a storage account deploying the script, changing the configuration run un the script and in the end open the power BI template in\n{ts:868} n this case the from Lake Power BI template and refresh it and you are good to go.\n...\nYeah\n...\nYeah, it's not on the logs.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://github.com/martinskeem/powerbi-powershell","title":"GitHub - martinskeem/powerbi-powershell: Various scripts and functions for harvesting Power BI meta data and automating Power BI tasks using API.","domain":"github.com","summary":"powerbi-powershell ** Public\n...\nVarious scripts and functions for harvesting Power BI meta data and automating Power BI tasks using API.\n...\n1. Create Azure AD app from https://portal.azure.com (see documentation).\nChose \"Native\" as the application type.\nSet Required Permissions for Power BI and with permissions which will at least support whatever you are planning to do.\nYou can also create an application from here, but not all permissions will be enabled by default (e.g. access to enumerate gateways).\n2. Azure PowerShell needs to be installed prior to running any of the scripts.\nInstall from here or here\n## Installing\nEihter clone this repository or install from PowerShell Gallery using this code:\n```\nInstall-Module -Name powerbi-powershell\n```\n...\nIt is possible to iterate Power BI entities (gateways, workspaces, users, datasets, refresh logs etc.) and extract this.\n...\n### Report gateway status to event log\nA typical production requirement might be to implement some sort of surveillance of the gateway.\nBelow sample will report current activity state of gateway (active, inactive) into the Application Event Log, which then, in turn could be monitored by a central function.\nBefore the first time the script is executed, the event log source must be created using:\n```\nNew-EventLog -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -LogName \"Application\"\n```\nOnce the source is created, below can be scheduled to run e.g. every 5 minutes after filling in the gateway name and details of the Azure AD app.\n```\n$gatewayName = \"\" # insert name of Gateway to check here.\n$clientId = \"\" # The ID of the Application ID of the App Registration in Azure AD\n...\nVarious scripts and functions for harvesting Power BI meta data and automating Power BI tasks using API.","date":"2017-11-29"},{"url":"https://gist.github.com/SQLvariant/fd3b77e597fc6e13118636bf0d682383","title":"Get the Governance Data You Need Out of Your Power BI Gateways with PowerShell","domain":"gist.github.com","summary":"Get the Governance Data You Need Out of Your Power BI Gateways with PowerShell\n...\n| |<# Make sure you have the modules installed.|\n|--|--|\n| |You only need the ImportExcel module if you want to export the results to Excel files.\n#>|\n| |Install-Module DataGateway|\n| |Install-Module ImportExcel|\n| | |\n| |<# Discovery #>|\n| |# All Gateway Clusters in your tenant.|\n| |Get-DataGatewayCluster -Scope Organization|\n| | |\n| |# All nodes of all Gateway Clusters in your tenant.|\n| |Get-DataGatewayCluster -Scope Organization | SELECT -ExpandProperty MemberGateways|\n| | |\n| |# All Data Sources for all Gateways you have Admin right to.|\n| |Get-DataGatewayCluster | Get-DataGatewayClusterDatasource|\n...\n| |foreach ($clusterDS in Get-DataGatewayCluster | Get-DataGatewayClusterDatasource)|\n...\n| |$a = Get-DataGatewayClusterDatasourceUser -GatewayClusterId $clusterDS.ClusterId -GatewayClusterDatasourceId $clusterDS.Id|\n...\n| |$a | Format-Table -Autosize|\n...\n| |within the same Excel file.\n#>|\n| |Get-DataGatewayCluster -Scope Organization ||\n| |Export-Excel -Path c:\\temp\\PPGateways.xlsx -WorksheetName DataGatewayClusters|","date":"2020-09-29"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-troubleshooting-solution-rui-romano","title":"A Power BI Gateway Monitoring & Troubleshooting Solution - LinkedIn","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"So, to ease the pain of these #powerbi admins I created a simple open-source solution that require admins to publish a simple & lightweight #powershell script to each gateway server that incrementally send & classify (by gatewayid, log type, timestamp) the gateway logs to a central storage account allowing admins to:\nIn the end you can just explore the logs using Azure Portal or Azure Storage Explorer:\nOr using a built-in Power BI template:\nDownload solution & setup instructions here\nData Engineer at Amazon7mo","date":"2022-06-26"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=963KPiL2PZk","title":"055 - On Premises Data Gateway Monitoring Status - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"{ts:129} you do need to download powershell 7 and powershell 7 is a prerequisite because\n{ts:136} of the power bi powershell command lines that we're going to use those have been written in powershell 7\n{ts:144} and as a result we need to make sure this is installed on our machine so here's where you can go ahead and\n{ts:150} grab the latest version of powershell and i will include a link in the description of this video\n...\n{ts:175} that service principle requirement now when you go ahead and set this up the most important thing to do here\n{ts:181} is you've got api permissions and you need the power bi service and then these are the two operations\n{ts:188} that we're going to be calling underneath the hood tenant.read.all and tenantreadwrite.all\n{ts:195} now do note these do require admin consent and for good reason when we run this we are going to run\n{ts:201} this using an organization scope so we are going to go ahead and discover\n{ts:206} all gateways in our environment that is certainly a requirement for all of this monitoring scenario\n{ts:213} itself another thing is and this will be covered in the\n{ts:219} actual powershell scripts that you do write but we are going to take advantage of\n{ts:224} this data gateway module now this is provided by microsoft and so\n{ts:229} that is what we need to do is we need to go ahead and install it because shortly here we're going to go\n{ts:234} ahead and import that as a part of our script and naturally it needs to be there in\n{ts:239} order for us to go ahead and use it once again more information at the following link i'll include that in the\n{ts:244} description itself once it's installed then we can go ahead and we can\n{ts:249} import it and this will be part of our overall scripts that we'll talk a little bit\n...\n{ts:268} inside of powershell now we're going to talk about two specifically the get data gateway\n{ts:274} cluster and the get data gateway cluster status and so let's talk a little bit about\n...\n{ts:311} can essentially discover all of the gateways that exist in our environment\n{ts:316} by using the get data gateway cluster command now this won't give us the status we have to use the\n{ts:324} id value that is returned from this call in order to actually get the status of a specific\n{ts:332} gateway instance itself also note that it is being called here as the cluster it's a single operation that\n...\n{ts:364} as well but as i mentioned we want to focus across the enterprise and then what we do need to do is feed\n{ts:369} in a gateway cluster id which was the same id that we talked about in the previous\n{ts:376} slide itself now when we make this call we will see a status in this case it'll be none which essentially means offline\n{ts:383} and then if a cluster is online we will see a value of live now in order to simplify\n...\n{ts:530} find and make that call to get the individual status and here we can see for this specific gateway\n{ts:536} there is a value of none so we're going to essentially loop through all of these gateways that\n{ts:542} are returned and then one by one check their individual status and then we'll be\n...\n{ts:652} the next thing we're going to do is we're going to go ahead and run our powershell script\n{ts:656} now this is one of the scripts that's available on my blog post and if we look at it we'll find it here\n{ts:663} it's going to be getgatewaylist.ps1 let's go ahead and edit it and now as part of this what we're going to do is\n{ts:671} pass in argument so basically our app id our secret and our tenant we're then going to construct a secret\n{ts:676} string because we naturally need to do that and we will go ahead and we will\n{ts:682} connect to the number one we'll connect we'll create a connection to the gateway service account using\n{ts:689} those values then what we'll do is we'll import the data gateway\n{ts:694} module and then we will go ahead and execute the get data gateway cluster operation and we're going to do this at\n{ts:701} the organization scope and we're going to output the all of that data to a file itself\n{ts:708} now when we call this gateway script here do note that i have this pwsh\n...\n{ts:748} so at this point we're going to output that and we're going to have basically our list of gateways\n{ts:753} i'm then going to go ahead and i'm going to split the file up based upon a new line\n{ts:759} because remember it was kind of ugly right we have all of these different sections\n{ts:763} and i need to be able to parse through it so what i'm going to do is i'm going to say for every new line\n{ts:768} basically split this up and it's going to become essentially a list now i'm going to go ahead and then\n{ts:777} launch i can create a list here just to manage some of the data and then what i can do is launch the\n{ts:782} excel so excel will that will be open and then what i'm going to do is for each row essentially i'm going to go\n{ts:788} ahead and get the current item text and what this is i'm just\n…\n{ts:843} second step which is something similar then what i'm going to do is check to see if this subtext\n{ts:849} is equal to id space if it is i know that i want to further parse that row and what i can do is i can split that\n{ts:856} row using a delimiter and what that is going to do is that is going to allow me to retrieve\n{ts:863} this value because we're going to basically take this row of text split it into two and then i can use the\n{ts:869} index to determine which portion of this essentially array i want to go ahead\n{ts:874} and and use so we're going to go ahead and do that and at this point we will have the id value\n{ts:880} for our gateway then what we're going to go ahead and do is call our second script which is going to be get the\n{ts:886} gateway list status and we're going to provide the value basically that value\n{ts:891} of of this id and we need to pass that into the secondary script and so when we do that\n{ts:900} we can go ahead and then you know essentially we're going to follow a very similar\n{ts:905} pattern now for each row because i know that right now i have an id i need to be able to capture that in\n...\n{ts:970} the powershell script here we're going to read the file and in this case that's that\n{ts:976} currentgatewaystatus.txt and here this is where we now need to parse this value\n{ts:984} and so once again we're going to follow a fairly similar pattern where we will read it we will then go\n…\n{ts:1006} get me the first 14 characters now first 14 characters equal cluster status space then let's go\n{ts:1013} split it again and we'll once again use a delimiter and then what we'll do is we will write\n{ts:1019} the value of the cluster name also into that excel spreadsheet uh sorry the cluster status into that\n{ts:1026} excel spreadsheet in this case it's column c now the last step in our loop um is we're then going to go ahead and\n{ts:1033} get the gateway name and we will go ahead and capture that from like the original uh text because\n{ts:1039} we've already processed the id and we have also got the subtext just in case\n{ts:1045} it is a name and so that's why we're going to then just go ahead and process name once again if we see that the name","date":"2020-10-25"},{"url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/PowerBI/comments/12ba9a7/reporting_on_power_bi_api_logs/","title":"Reporting on Power BI API Logs","domain":"www.reddit.com","summary":"There are a few solutions out there but this, in my opinion, is the best:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbimonitor\nIt's built by a colleague of mine and is used by some of the largest Power BI customers in the world.\nRui's solution for monitoring gateway logs is excellent too: https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nThanks, will check it out."},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.","date":"2024-10-27"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"PerfMon data gateway performance counter collector script","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://sqlserverblogforum.com/integrate-ssrs-with-perfmon-dashboard-performance-of-database-graph-using-ssrs-collect-perfmon-and-automate-it-load-to-sql-database-and-generate-reports/","title":"3 Comments","domain":"sqlserverblogforum.com","summary":"- Create two notepad and save it any drive (performance.lst, server.lst).\nThe performance notepad will have counter details and server notepad will have server name\n- Create one CMD file called createlogs.cmd.\nCreatelogs cmd will have script to create perfmon data collector in a single shot with all specified counter and in the perfmon tool.\nThis will create folder and place the BLG counter files in the path.\n(The counter details and CMD scripts are bottom of this post)\n- Once data collected, convert to csv for easy read.\n- Load the CSV to data table using script to read and make a easy trend and analysis.\n- Additionally, if you have SSRS you can configure to view data as graph trend..\n...\n__Setup PerfMon and collect data__\nThis can be done by perfmon windows utility.\nVerify SQL performance logging counters are already installed on — run — perfmon.msc\nEnsure there are a few gigabytes free on the D: drive.\nThe createlogs.cmd script will try in D (You can change the D: drive to any drive, just use ctrl+H to repalce in 11 place – D: in 9 places, D$ in one place and set DESTDRV=drive name)\nSave a notepad as server.lst that should contain target servers list\nSave a notepad as performance.lst that should contain all important counters\nSave createlogs.cmd as cmd.\nCopy all three files into server with all name, counter and code.\nOpen command prompt as administrator– (Change the directory CD and dir)\n…\nYou will see all files.Now type createlogs.cmd\nexample – C:\\Users\\muthu\\Desktop\\SQLDBA\\Perfmon>createlogs.cmd\nEnter it.\nThis will create folder and schedule in perfmon data collector.\nmake sure to verify all.\nVerification: Open perfmon.msc and verify counters are green and working good.\n...\n*Look on the perfmon GUI and make sure you have processor, Disk, Memory and network counters, if anything missed, you can add it here as well.*\n...\n**createlogs.cmd** — Save this as a CMD file — Given by my windows team.\n...\n@echo off echo ************************************************************************** echo * CREATELOGS.CMD echo * echo * This command configures a perfmon collection log on the target server(s) echo * specified in server.lst.\necho * The configured counters are taken from performance.lst unless echo * a filename is provided on the command line.\necho * echo *\nNote: You must have administrator rights on the target servers.\necho ************************************************************************** SET COUNTERFILE=performance.lst if not \"%1\" == \"\" set COUNTERFILE=%1 if \"%2\" == \"\" set LISTFILE=server.lst if not \"%2\" == \"\" set LISTFILE=%2 if not exist \"%COUNTERFILE%\" goto ERRBadFile for /F %%i in (%LISTFILE%) do call :CreateLog %%i %COUNTERFILE% goto End :ERRBadFile echo ERROR: Performance counter file could not be found!\ngoto End :CreateLog rem If no server specified then quit if \"%1\" == \"\" echo ERROR: No target server specified if \"%1\" == \"\" goto End if \"%2\" == \"\" echo ERROR: No performance counters specified if \"%2\" == \"\" goto End rem Attach to target server (must have admininistrator\n...\nNote: When server reboots your data collector could stop, you need to use task scheduler to start – logman start “ServerName_Test” –1) create a basic task 2) schedule when the computer starts 3) In the program/script copy and paste “logman” and in Add aruguments — writename of the job i.e. start “ServerName_Test”‘ click yes.\n...\nEx: relog F:\\logs\\perflogs\\csv\\server_test_12100514.csv -f SQL -o SQL:DBA_Test!DBA_Perfmon\n…\n*We can do this by using PowerShell or windows utility relog.exe and it will create tables and load a data from CSV.\n*We need an ODBC user DSN to connect the database to loading a data using relog.exe.\nCreate a database called\n**DBA_Perfmon**.\n...\n1.Copy and paste data from table results with column and replace \\ 2.\nChange COLUMN date format to “right date and time” 3.\nSelect A2 and click Insert menu select PivotTable and choose pivot chart 4.\nClick ok 5.\nAxis Field “date” 6.\nIn values field “add counter value” in the value select value field settings and choose max .If needed filtering it the counter names but good to do this each counter separately.\n...\nYou can check the report by using the SP- EXEC USP_RS_PERFMON ‘\\win-pc’,@OBJECTNAME=’PhysicalDisk’,@COUNTERNAME=’Avg.","date":"2019-11-02"},{"url":"https://github.com/Petar-T/PerfMon-collector","title":"GitHub - Petar-T/PerfMon-collector: PowerShell script to create and collect configurable list of performance counters, extract it and PowerBI report to show collected data","domain":"github.com","summary":"# PerfMon-collector\nPowerShell script to create and collect configurable list of performance counters, extract it and PowerBI report to show collected data\n## Usage\n```\nrun PerfMon_Setup.ps1\n#to create performance collector\nrun PerfMon_Fix_Header.ps1\n#to standardize header of file\nOpen Perfmon_Dashboard.pbix and load file\n#to analyze data\n```\n...\n```\nst=>start: create collector\nop=>operation: collect data (few hrs)\ncond=>condition: Successful Yes or No?\nop2=>operation: extract file\nop3=>operation: adjust header\ne=>end: import .pbix to PowerBI desktop and reload\nst->op->cond\ncond(yes)->op2\nop2->op3\ncond(no)->op\nop3->e\n```","date":"2019-12-09"},{"url":"https://sveroadev.wordpress.com/2014/05/06/perfmon-create-collector-set-with-powershell/","title":"Perfmon – create collector set with powershell","domain":"sveroadev.wordpress.com","summary":"For the last year I have set up a number of collector sets, and found it useful to organize these into monthly folders with “YYYY-MM and set up one BLG for each day.\nI normally set up a schedule with 12-16 hours, starting at 06:00 and ending around 22:00.\nThis enables me to analyse performance issues in normal work hours, plus/minus a few hours.\nI only set an “All-Day” schedule if I want to monitor the server performance during backup and nightly maintenance.\nThe following script is used to create the data collector set and start it.\nDownload full script here.\n>\nParam( [string]$name = \"MyCollectorSet\" ) $datacollectorset = New-Object -COM Pla.DataCollectorSet $datacollectorset.DisplayName = $name; $datacollectorset.Duration = 50400 ; $datacollectorset.SubdirectoryFormat = 1 ; $\ndatacollectorset.SubdirectoryFormatPattern = \"yyyy\\-MM\"; $datacollectorset.RootPath = \"%systemdrive%\\PerfLogs\\Admin\\\" + $name ; $DataCollector = $datacollectorset.DataCollectors.CreateDataCollector(0) $DataCollector.FileName = $name + \"_\"; $DataCollector.FileNameFormat = 0x1 ; $DataCollector.FileNameFormatPattern = \"yyyy\\-MM\\-dd\"; $DataCollector.SampleInterval = 15 $DataCollector.LogAppend = $true; $counters = @( \"\\PhysicalDisk\\Avg.\nDisk Sec/Read\", \"\\PhysicalDisk\\Avg.\nDisk Sec/Write\", \"\\PhysicalDisk\\Avg.\nDisk Queue Length\", \"\\Memory\\Available MBytes\", \"\\Processor(_Total)\\% Processor Time\", \"\\System\\Processor Queue Length\" ) ; $DataCollector.PerformanceCounters = $counters $StartDate = [DateTime]('2013-01-01 06:00:00'); $NewSchedule\n= $datacollectorset.schedules.CreateSchedule() $NewSchedule.Days = 127 $NewSchedule.StartDate = $StartDate $NewSchedule.StartTime = $StartDate try { $datacollectorset.schedules.Add($NewSchedule) $datacollectorset.DataCollectors.Add($DataCollector) $datacollectorset.Commit(\"$name\" , $null , 0x0003) | Out-Null $datacollectorset.start($false); } catch [Exception] { Write-Host \"Exception Caught: \" $_.Exception -ForegroundColor Red return }\nIf your SYSTEM account is not member of the local groups “Performance Log Users” and “Performance Monitor Users”, the following powershell commands can be run to add these memberships:\n> $group = [ADSI]\"WinNT://./Performance Log Users,group\" $group.Add(\"WinNT://SYSTEM,user\"); $group = [ADSI]\"WinNT://./Performance Monitor Users,group\" $group.Add(\"WinNT://SYSTEM,user\");\nWhen a performance monitor data collector set is screated, this can be found under Task Scheduler under “\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PLA”.\nIt is very useful to make some minor changes to the settings of the data collector set that is not available from Performance monitor.\nIf you want the data collector set to automatically start after server reboot, you should add a “At startup” trigger to the scheduled task.\n...\n“\nThe $counters is just an array of strings that contains the performance counters that you want to collect data\n...\nYou can stop and start data collector set from Perfmon.exe","date":"2014-05-06"},{"url":"https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/158292/creating-a-performance-monitor-counter-s.html","title":"Creating a Performance Monitor counter set for Notification ...","domain":"knowledge.broadcom.com","summary":"## Create a performance monitor data collector from the attached template\n1. Run 'perfmon.msc'\n2. Expand 'Data Collector Sets', and right click on 'User Defined'\n3. New --> Data Collector Set\n4. Put in the name of the new collection and make sure that 'Create from a template' is checked then hit 'next'\n5. Hit the 'Browse' button and select the XML file attached to this document and then click the 'Finish' button\n6. Now you can right-click on the new user defined data collector set and select 'start'\n## Create a performance monitor data collector manually\n1. Run 'perfmon.msc'\n2. Expand 'Data Collector Sets', and right-click on 'User Defined'\n3. New --> Data Collector Set\n4. Put in the name of the new collection and make sure that 'Create manually (Advanced)' is checked then hit 'next'\n5. Under 'Create data logs' make sure to check 'Performance counter' then hit 'Next'\n6. Click the 'Add' button and select the counters you would like then hit 'OK'\n7. Next hit the 'Finish' button\n8. Next expand 'User Defined' in the left pane and left click on the new user defined data collector to highlight it.\nThen in the right pane right-click on the datacollector and select 'properties'\n9. Make sure under 'log format' select 'Binary' and then click 'apply' and then 'ok'\n10. Now you can right-click on the new user defined data collector set and select 'start'\n**Note:** The file that perfmon creates will be stored under C:\\Perflogs\\etc... by default\n**Note**: The file format that will be most useful will end in .blg (Binary under log format!) and can be opened by perfmon on our machines."},{"url":"https://community.spiceworks.com/topic/2248192-how-do-i-ensure-that-perfmon-isn-t-going-to-harm-performance","title":"How do I ensure that perfmon isn't going to harm performance?","domain":"community.spiceworks.com","summary":"Performance Monitor collects data every second by default.\nI use customized User Defined Collector sets that collect data based on the symptom.\nIf I want to collect data over a period of days to see if the server gets overloaded, I will collect almost everything once a minute.\nThis is saved to disk in a file.\n...\nI'll give you my method.\nFirst I have a file with all the counters I want to collect.\nWe'll call it WFE.txt for a web server.\n\\ASP.NET(*)\\*\n\\ASP.NET v2.0.50727\\*\n\\ASP.NET Apps v2.0.50727(*)\\*\n\\.NET CLR Networking(*)\\*\n\\.NET CLR Memory(*)\\*\n\\.NET CLR Exception(*)\\*\n\\.NET CLR Loading(_Global_)\\*\n\\.NET CLR LocksAndThreads(_Global_)\\*\n\\.NET Data Provider for SqlServer(*)\\*\n\\Paging File(*)\\*\n\\Processor(*)\\*\n\\Process(*)\\*\n\\LogicalDisk(*)\\*\n\\Memory\\*\n\\Network Interface(*)\\*\n\\PhysicalDisk(*)\\*\n\\SharePoint Publishing Cache(*)\\*\n\\System\\*\n\\TCPv4\\*\n\\TCPV6\\*\n\\Web Service(*)\\*\n\\Web Service Cache\\*\nThen I run the command which installs the new User Defined Collector set into Performance Monitor.\nlogman create counter Perf_Baseline -s %COMPUTERNAME% -o C:\\PerfLogs\\Perf_Baseline_%Computername%.blg -f bincirc -v mmddhhmm -cf WFE.txt -si 00:01:00 -max 700\nThis creates a Perfmon Collector set called Perf_Baseline.\nIt names the log files and saves them to the C:\\Perflogs directory.\nIt is a circular file with a 700 MB size limit which will hold 4 to 7 days of data.\nIt collects data once per minute.\nThen you start your data collection.\nlogman start Perf_Baseline\nYou need to stop Perfmon normally so you do not corrupt the data.\nIf the server reboots the data is corrupted and no good.\nlogman stop Perf_Baseline\nYou can either read the log file with Performance Monitor or you can chart it with the PAL Tool Opens a new window"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/zh-tw/archive/blogs/web_performance_blog/performance-counter-collection","title":"Performance Counter Collection","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"Windows ships with the Performance Monitor tool, or PerfMon for short.\nThis GUI allows you to view live performance counter data on either the local machine or on remote systems.\nSimply run `perfmon` and navigate to Performance->Monitoring Tools->Performance Monitor.\nRight click to add or remove counters.\nPress ‘Ctrl+G’ to switch the graph type.\nReport View is handy when monitoring multiple counters.\n...\nPerfecto is a Data Collector Set that plugs into PerfMon, and was created by Jose Reyes.\nThis is a quick, simple way to collect ASP.NET, IIS, CLR and system counters which give a good overview of ASP.NET performance.\nLogMan is a command line tool for managing PerfMon Data Collector Sets.\nTry the following:\n1. Download Perfecto, unzip and run the setup script\n2. Run `logman start –n “Service\\ASPNET Perfecto”`\n3. Make a few web requests to localhost\n4. Run `logman stop –n “Service\\ASPNET Perfecto”`\n5. View the report at \"%SystemDrive%\\PerfLogs\\Admin\\ASPNET Perfecto\\000001\\report.html”\nDon’t be afraid to customize Perfecto to your needs: add performance counters, change the report directory, etc.\nRight click ‘ASPNET Perfecto’ to edit the properties in PerfMon.\nRight click again and Save Template… to create an updated version of Perfecto.DataCollectorSet.xml.\n...\nFor basic counter collections, you can use the TypePerf command-line tool.\nIt can be combined with the TList command in order to collect counters which are specific to a given process.\nFor example, you can collect the Processor Time for all processes by running:\n> `for /t “tokens=2 delims=.\n“ %a in (‘tlist’) do typeperf –sc 1 “Process(%a)\\% Processor Time”\n...\nWCat also supports the collection of performance counters.\nThis is our primary way of collecting performance counters since most of our scenarios use WCat.\nTake a look at our settings.ubr file to see which counters we track:","date":"2012-02-13"},{"url":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/25485767/get-the-counters-when-the-windows-performance-monitor-perfmon-data-collecter-i/25491944","title":"Get the counters when the Windows Performance Monitor (perfmon) Data Collecter is running","domain":"stackoverflow.com","summary":"If you can run powershell scripts you may use\n`get-counter`and\n`export-counter`cmdlets :\nyou can see a simple example here : http://technet.microsoft.com/en-us/magazine/ee872428.aspx\nIf you are on a localised windows (not english), you will have to get the correct counter name before.\nFor example in my french windows version it will be :\n`Get-counter -ListSet *`\nthis will display all the available counterset.\nI can see there is a set named \"processeur\" wich is the french word for CPU.\nSo now i can get the available counter for this set like this:\n`Get-counter -ListSet \"processeur\" |select -expand counter`\nit gives me the result :\nNow I can use one or more of these counter like this :\nget all the counters :\n`Get-counter -ListSet \"processeur\" |select -expand counter | foreach{ get-counter $_ -SampleInterval 2 -MaxSamples 10}`\nget inactivity counter :\n`Get-counter -ListSet \"processeur\" |select -expand counter |where {$_ -match \"inactivité\"} | foreach{ get-counter $_ -SampleInterval 2 -MaxSamples 10}`\nAt least, you can pipe the result to\n`export-counter`in order to save it to a file (CSV, TSV, or BLG).\nHope that's help\nHere is how to use\n`export-counter`to get a CSV and avoid having to do the BLG to CSV conversion (\nobviously modifying the command to fit your needs):","date":"2014-08-25"},{"url":"https://github.com/albugrimenko/PerfMon","title":"GitHub - albugrimenko/PerfMon: Performance Monitor data collection, storage and reports: power shell scrips, SQL, HTML","domain":"github.com","summary":"Windows Performance Monitor data collection, storage and reports: power shell scrips, SQL, HTML.\nSet up Windows Performance Monitor to collect all sorts of metrics on daily basis.\nRun power shell script to collect data from multiple servers and store it in the centralized database.\nUse provided stored procs to visually present data.\nAll counters are grouped by Server, Metric Set and Metric.\nData values averaged with 5 sec time interval.\n...\n## Setup\n1. SQL Server database using deployment scripts provided in the DB folder.\n2. Setup performance monitor data collection on each server you want to monitor.\nYou can set whatever counters you think are important for each server.\nDetailed information on how to set up monitoring is in the *Perfmon-setup-Win2008R2-and-above-20171106.pdf*\n3. Make sure that data collected in the local folder and can be accessed remotely from the central server by the user you are planning to run data collection on daily basis.\n4. List all servers and corresponding shared folders in the *PerfmonToSQL_AB.servers* file.\nFormat is simple: Server name|Remote location\n5. Run power shell script to import data from all servers.\n...\n- Run power shell script daily/hourly or by whatever schedule you need.\n- Run stored procedure srv_ComputeStats daily, to make sure that all statistics are fresh.\nRead “Collected metric values analysis” below for details.","date":"2018-05-20"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Fabric gateway monitoring Azure Log Analytics","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-fabric/service-fabric-diagnostics-event-analysis-oms","title":"Azure Service Fabric Event Analysis with Azure Monitor logs","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"Azure Monitor logs collects and analyzes telemetry from applications and services hosted in the cloud and provides analysis tools to help you maximize their availability and performance.\n...\n## Access the Service Fabric Analytics solution\nIn the Azure portal, go to the resource group in which you created the Service Fabric Analytics solution.\nSelect the resource **ServiceFabric<nameOfOMSWorkspace>**.\nIn `Summary`, you will see tiles in the form of a graph for each of the solutions enabled, including one for Service Fabric.\nSelect the **Service Fabric** graph to continue to the Service Fabric Analytics solution.\n...\nOn the Service Fabric Analytics page, select the graph for **Service Fabric Events**.\nSelect **List** to view the events in a list.\nOnce here, you see all the system events that have been collected.\n...\nAlternatively, you can select the magnifying glass on the left and use the Kusto query language to find what you're looking for.\n...\nOn the Service Fabric Analytics page, select the graph for **Reliable Services**.\nSelect **List** to view the events in a list.\n...\n- To enable infrastructure monitoring i.e. performance counters, head over to adding the Log Analytics agent.\nThe agent collects performance counters and adds them to your existing workspace.\n- For on-premises clusters, Azure Monitor logs offers a Gateway (HTTP Forward Proxy) that can be used to send data to Azure Monitor logs.\nRead more about that in Connecting computers without Internet access to Azure Monitor logs using the Log Analytics gateway.\n- Configure automated alerting to aid in detection and diagnostics.\n- Get familiarized with the log search and querying features offered as part of Azure Monitor logs.","date":"2026-03-22"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Data-Engineering/Connecting-to-Azure-Log-Analytics-for-my-Fabric-Notebook/m-p/4927188","title":"Re: Connecting to Azure Log Analytics for my Fabric Notebook","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"You can't use Workspace Identity to authenticate to Azure Log Analytics - already covered in another thread.\nFabric workspace identities cannot currently acquire tokens for the Azure Monitor (Log Analytics) resource provider.\n...\nYou can however use a service principal (Azure AD App Registration) and use the code below -\n```\nfrom azure.identity import ClientSecretCredential\nfrom azure.monitor.query import LogsQueryClient\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nimport pandas as pd\ntenant_id = \"<TENANT-ID>\"\nclient_id = \"<CLIENT-ID>\"\nclient_secret = \"<CLIENT-SECRET>\"\ncredential = ClientSecretCredential(\ntenant_id=tenant_id,\nclient_id=client_id,\nclient_secret=client_secret\n)\nclient = LogsQueryClient(credential)\nkql_query = \"\"\"\nSigninLogs\n| where TimeGenerated >= ago(7d)\n| where AppDisplayName == \"Windows Sign In\"\n\"\"\"\n```\n...\nIf you're running the notebook manually, notebookutils will be using your account as the security context, so you will need to make sure that your account has access to the log analytics workspace.","date":"2026-01-26"},{"url":"https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/azurepaasblog/standalone-service-fabric-integration-with-azure-log-analytics/902411","title":"Standalone Service Fabric Integration with Azure Log Analytics | Microsoft Community Hub","domain":"techcommunity.microsoft.com","summary":"The Azure Log Analytics agent, previously referred to as the Microsoft Monitoring Agent (MMA) or OMS agent.\nThese agents attach to an Azure Monitor and store collected log data from different sources in your Log Analytics workspace, as well as any unique logs or metrics as defined in a monitoring solution.\nIn three simple steps, in this article we will configure Service Fabric Log Analytics workspace to retrieve performance counter for Service Fabric (performance /infrastructure monitoring) and events for system, application and Service Fabric (cluster/platform monitoring).\nFurther we will create alert in Azure Log Analytics when node gets down.\nIn other option, you can achieved this via EventFlow (https://github.com/Azure/diagnostics-EventFlow).\nWrite a small app that listens for Service Fabric ETW events and forwards them to app insights / event hub / more options.\nFor Application logs you have to write you own code and generate logs.\nThese can also be pushed to Log Analytics using custom logs.\nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/platform/data-sources-custom-logs .\n**1) Create the Service Fabric Log Analytics workspace**\nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-fabric/service-fabric-diagnostics-oms-setup#deploy-a-log-analytics-workspace-by-using-azure-marketplace\n**2) Install the agent on-premises Virtual Machines** (If you have a 3 Node/ 5 Node cluster then this agent needs to be installed on each Virtual Machine).\nDownload:\nWindows 64-bit agent - https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=828603\nOnce the agent is downloaded, you can go to install the agent using the setup wizard\nCheck if connected:\n*Note* * that even after installing and connecting the agent to the Log Analytics workspace, to get certain performance counter/events related to Service Fabric, you would need to manually add some additional performance counters/events in the Log analytics workspace.\nFor this refer to step 3.*\nCheck Service Fabric performance counters on-premise virtual machine.\nList of performance counter https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-fabric/service-fabric-diagnostics-event-generation-perf\nCheck Service Fabric events on-premise virtual machine.\nList of events https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-fabric/service-fabric-diagnostics-event-generation-operational\n**3) Config the Azure Log Analytics workspace on the Azure Portal**\nYou would need to go to Portal > Log Analytics Worksapce > “Workspace” > Advanced Settings > Data > Windows Performance data.\nAfter that in the “Collect the following performance counters > Search bar, you would need to add the below counters” or any other counter you wish to monitor.\nThey may not be shown in the auto-complete, but you can add them and save the changes.\nService Fabric Naming Gateway(*)\\Client Connections\nService Fabric Reconfiguration Agent(*)\\# FTs\nService Fabric Native Store\\”CounterName”\nIn addition, you can also send event viewer traces (ETW) for application and system events and performance counter refer to below screens.\nMore details at : https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/platform/agent-data-sources#configuring-data-sources.\nFurther you can add Service Fabric events for Monitoring.\nAdd Service Fabric Events:\nMicrosoft-ServiceFabric/Admin\nMicrosoft-ServiceFabric/Operational\n**Validation**:\nWait for sometime around 15-20 minutes and run the below query in the Log Search : *Perf | where ObjectName contains “Service Fabric Reconfiguration Agent” * and check for other counter*.*\n**Performance Counter (** performance /infrastructure monitoring)**:**\n**Events (** cluster/platform monitoring)**:**\n**Configure an Alert: **\nClick \"New Alert Rule\" at the top and now anytime an event arrives based on this query, you will receive an alert in your chosen method of communication.\nThis will create an alert when a node goes down:\nEvent\n| where EventLevel == 1 and ParameterXml contains \"NodeDown\" and EventLog == \"Microsoft-ServiceFabric/Operational\"","date":"2019-10-09"},{"url":"https://blog.fabric.microsoft.com/en/blog/unlocking-real-time-operational-intelligence-azure-monitor-logs-integration-in-fabric-via-eventstream?ft=All","title":"Unlocking Real-Time Operational Intelligence: Azure Monitor Logs ...","domain":"blog.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"Today we are introducing the integration of Azure Monitor Diagnostic Logs directly into Fabric via Eventstream, enabling seamless ingestion of Azure resource logs into Fabric-native analytics workflows.\n...\nThis new integration streamlines the process by allowing direct ingestion of Azure Monitor logs into Fabric Eventstream, reducing setup friction and improving operational inefficiency to gain holistic insights.\n...\n- Efficiently route logs and metrics of Azure resources from Azure into Fabric.\n- Transform data logs for immediate use without a single line of code.\n- Combine diagnostic logs with other data sources for enriched, cross-vertical operational insights.\n- Ingest, visualize and act on data in real time, all within a unified platform.\n...\nThe integration utilizes Fabric’s Eventstream to provide a streamlined workflow for ingesting Azure Monitor logs.\nUsers begin by navigating to the Real-Time Hub within Fabric and selecting the Azure Diagnostics data source card.\nThis interface allows users to efficiently search for specific Azure resources and apply filters based on source type, subscription, resource group, or region, making it simple to locate and view detailed resource attributes.\nOnce the desired resources are identified, users can initiate the end-to-end ingestion flow directly from the selected Azure resource.\nWithin the workflow, users are able to select which logs and metrics to import into Fabric and may also configure the Eventhouse destination for the processed logs.\nThis process enables the direct routing of Azure Monitor logs into Eventstream, where the logs can be transformed as needed.\nSubsequently, the transformed data can be sent to Fabric’s native destinations, including Eventhouse, Activator, and custom endpoints, supporting a seamless analytics workflow within the Fabric platform.\n...\nFollow the steps in the Stream Azure Diagnostics to Fabric documentation and start unlocking real-time operational intelligence in Fabric today!","date":"2025-09-22"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-platform/How-to-Monitor-Usage-Across-Multiple-Virtual-Network-Data/m-p/4864674","title":"Re: How to Monitor Usage Across Multiple Virtual N...","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"You can see the VNet Gateway CUs in the Fabric Capacity Metrics App.\n...\nAt the moment, **Microsoft Fabric does not expose CU (Capacity Unit) consumption at the individual data gateway level**, whether for on-premises or virtual network data gateways.\nHowever, here’s what you *can* do:\n1) Install the Fabric Capacity Metrics App to monitor CU by **workspace and workload type**\nDocs :\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/metrics-app\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/metrics-app-install?tabs=1st\n2) **Gateway activity – Audit and diagnostic logs**\nWhile CU-level data isn’t tracked, you can **monitor connection and query activity** per gateway via:\n- **Microsoft Fabric Audit Logs** (Power BI Admin → Audit log search)\n- **Gateway Diagnostic Logs** from the Power Platform Admin Center or Azure Log Analytics\nThese include:\n- Number of queries and connections\n- Average duration\n- Failed vs successful requests\nThis lets you indirectly identify if a gateway is overused or idle.\nDocs :\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","date":"2025-11-07"},{"url":"https://docs.azure.cn/en-us/service-fabric/service-fabric-diagnostics-oms-agent","title":"Performance Monitoring with Azure Monitor logs","domain":"docs.azure.cn","summary":"This article covers the steps to add the Log Analytics agent as a virtual machine scale set extension to your cluster, and connect it to your existing Azure Log Analytics workspace.\nThis enables collecting diagnostics data about containers, applications, and performance monitoring.\nBy adding it as an extension to the virtual machine scale set resource, Azure Resource Manager ensures that it gets installed on every node, even when scaling the cluster.\n...\nThis article assumes that you have an Azure Log Analytics workspace already set up.\nIf you do not, head over to Set up Azure Monitor logs.\n## Add the agent extension via Azure CLI\nThe best way to add the Log Analytics agent to your cluster is via the virtual machine scale set APIs available with the Azure CLI.\nIf you don't have Azure CLI set up yet, head over to Azure portal and open up the Azure CLI.\nOnce your local Shell is requested, make sure you work in the same subscription as your resource.\nCheck this with\n`az account show`and make sure the \"name\" value matches that of your cluster's subscription.\nIn the Portal, navigate to the resource group where your Log Analytics workspace is located.\nSelect the log analytics resource (the type of the resource will be Log Analytics workspace).\nOnce you are at the resource overview page, select\n**Advanced Settings**under the Settings section on the left menu.\nSelect\n**Windows Servers**if you're standing up a Windows cluster, and **Linux Servers**if you're creating a Linux cluster.\nThis page shows your\n`workspace ID`and\n`workspace key`(listed as Primary Key in the portal).\nYou need both for the next step.\nRun the command to install the Log Analytics agent onto your cluster, using the\n`vmss extension set`API:\nFor a Windows cluster:\n`az vmss extension set --name MicrosoftMonitoringAgent --publisher Microsoft.EnterpriseCloud.Monitoring --resource-group <nameOfResourceGroup> --vmss-name <nameOfNodeType> --settings \"{'workspaceId':'<Log AnalyticsworkspaceId>'}\" --protected-settings \"{'workspaceKey':'<Log AnalyticsworkspaceKey>'}\"`\nFor a Linux cluster:\n`az vmss extension set --name OmsAgentForLinux --publisher Microsoft.EnterpriseCloud.Monitoring --resource-group <nameOfResourceGroup> --vmss-name <nameOfNodeType> --settings \"{'workspaceId':'<Log AnalyticsworkspaceId>'}\" --protected-settings \"{'workspaceKey':'<Log AnalyticsworkspaceKey>'}\"`\n...\nThis should take less than 15 min to successfully add the agent to your nodes.\nYou can verify that the agents have been added by using the\n`az vmss extension list`API:\n`az vmss extension list --resource-group <nameOfResourceGroup> --vmss-name <nameOfNodeType>`\n## Add the agent via the Resource Manager template\nSample Resource Manager templates that deploy an Azure Log Analytics workspace and add an agent to each of your nodes is available for Windows or Linux.\nYou can download and modify this template to deploy a cluster that best suits your needs.\n...\nNow that you have added the Log Analytics agent, head on over to the Log Analytics portal to choose which performance counters you'd like to collect.\nIn the Azure portal, go to the resource group in which you created the Service Fabric Analytics solution.\nSelect\n**ServiceFabric<nameOfLog AnalyticsWorkspace>**.\nSelect\n**Log Analytics**.\nSelect\n**Advanced Settings**.\nSelect\n**Data**, then choose **Windows or Linux Performance Counters**.\nThere is a list of default counters you can choose to enable and you can set the interval for collection too.\nYou can also add additional performance counters to collect.\nThe proper format is referenced in this article.\nSelect\n**Save**, then choose **OK**.\nClose the Advanced Settings blade.\nUnder the General heading, select\n**Workspace summary**.\nYou'll see tiles in the form of a graph for each of the solutions enabled, including one for Service Fabric.\nSelect the\n**Service Fabric**graph to continue to the Service Fabric Analytics solution.\nYou'll see a few tiles with graphs on operational channel and reliable services events.\nThe graphical representation of the data flowing in for the counters you have selected appear under Node Metrics.\nSelect on a Container Metric graph to see additional details.\nYou can also query on performance counter data similarly to cluster events and filter on the nodes, perf counter name, and values using the Kusto query language.\n## Next steps\n- Collect relevant performance counters.\nTo configure the Log Analytics agent to collect specific performance counters, review configuring data sources.\n- Configure Azure Monitor logs to set up automated alerting to aid in detecting and diagnostics\n- As an alternative you can collect performance counters through Azure Diagnostics extension and send them to Application Insights","date":"2026-03-30"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YCWY7-xICBU","title":"Real-Time Fabric Monitoring: A Step-by-Step Guide for Beginners!","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"🕒  In this tutorial, Manuel explores the built-in Real-Time Intelligence (RTI) monitoring for Fabric workspaces!\nLearn how to easily enable RTI monitoring with a single toggle in Workspace Settings → Monitoring.\n🔧\n✅ What you'll learn:\n➡️ Create and view key tables like SemanticModelLogs and EventhouseMetrics\n➡️ Query with Kusto Query Language (KQL) 🔍\n...\nAnd you\n{ts:46} might be thinking, wait, that's not new.\nI know there's an option for me to connect to Azure Log Analytics, like tie\n{ts:50} this to an Azure Log Analytics workspace.\nIt's actually not that.\nAnd you may have noticed this as well, but\n{ts:55} we're going to be talking about leveraging real-time intelligence so that we can do monitoring on our actual\n{ts:62} workspaces.\nSo, join me as we dive into this new feature which is built right into a workspace so that we can create\n{ts:69} and add basically a streaming connection to monitor what's going on in our workspaces.\n...\nThe monitoring sets preview is coming soon and now it is available to us inside of a workspace.\n...\nRight in this workspace, I'm simply going to go\n{ts:176} up to the corner in the right hand side where we go to the workspace settings.\n...\nAnd then yes, under Azure connections, this\n{ts:186} is historically where you could tie basically this workspace to an Azure log analytics workspace.\nSo you can see that\n{ts:192} you can send usage and performance logs and then you could query it there.\nAnd actually Azure log analytics can also\n...\n{ts:202} going to be talking about the monitoring option that you can find right here.\nAnd you can see this workspace.\nI've already\n{ts:207} enabled this, but really all you would do is turn on this switch.\nThere's an option, a little green button that says\n{ts:213} event house.\nThat's it.\nYou literally create the button.\nIf I go over to another workspace real quick, just so we\n{ts:218} can see this, we'll go to this one right here.\nAnd if I go to workspace settings, monitoring, there it is.\nAnd you can\n{ts:224} read it says to monitor workspace activity, add a readonly monitoring event house that includes a KQL database\n{ts:230} to store the data in logs, which you can then query, right?\nSo you can go into this database.\nYou can go to the query\n{ts:236} editor and yeah, you can do this.\nSo it just literally you click that button.\nThat's it.","date":"2025-10-30"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEuctpVzYAk","title":"Monitoring your Azure Data Platform with Fabric","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Angel Abundez of DesignMind will show you a quick way to start monitoring Azure Data Factory and Azure Data Lake Storage with Microsoft Fabric.\nYou will learn about Azure Monitoring, Logging Workspace Analytics, Kusto SQL, and a useful Fabric monitoring solution.\n...\n{ts:660} monitors that I that was my best friend was the ADF monitor but it turns out that Azure has a ton of monitoring built\n{ts:669} into it right out of the box there's um Azure monitor which um uh you know behind Azure monitor inside of azure\n{ts:679} there's uh what I'll talk about in a little bit uh that uh it's collecting logs so you spin up um any resource in\n{ts:688} azure there's a log for that somebody logs into Azure into the Azure portal there's a log for that so Azure monitor\n{ts:696} um is kind of like the central Hub of of all of azure for all your monitoring needs um now we were using data bricks\n…\n...\n{ts:1061} powerbi so um I was looking for something like that so in comes log workspace analytics log workspace\n{ts:1071} analytics is the kind of the storage engine behind Azure monitor um every anything in Azure um that can be\n{ts:1082} monitored VMS Azure data Lake storage Azure data Factory logic apps um uh uh everything app Services um it can all\n{ts:1095} all those logs can be dumped into log workspace analytics um so it turns out um whoops\n...\n{ts:1336} Azure resource um also in the diagnostic settings in Azure data Factory you have to change um the diagnostic setting\n{ts:1346} there's this there's a setting um that you have to set to Resource specific so that you get you can get access to this\n...\n{ts:1685} could use this in fabric using data flows is if you connect directly to log workspace analytics um you can expand","date":"2024-08-29"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"site:github.com Power BI gateway monitoring script","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://github.com/ecotte/Fabric-Monitoring-RTI","title":"ecotte/Fabric-Monitoring-RTI - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"ecotte / **\nFabric-Monitoring-RTI ** Public\n...\nTo address this, a solution has been developed based on Fabric Real-Time Intelligence (RTI).\nThis solution extracts information from audit logs through the API, gateway locks via a script included in the solution, and the inventory of the whole platform through the API.\n...\nTo implement this solution, we have some step to follow.\nThis steps will cover the creation of all the items in the previous architecture and the script in the Gateway Nodes.\nWe can find the following steps needs to be done:\n...\nCreate a workspace and import the Platform Monitoring Setup Notebook.\nFollow the instructions for the first run.\nIf you change the variable \"FIRST_RUN\" to \"False\" the script will update the Eventhouse definition and notebooks.\n...\n## Script deployment and setup in the gateway nodes (Optional)\nThese are available scripts to retrieve and process logs from on-premises gateways.\nThese scripts help in managing and processing logs.\nYou can find the gateway scripts in the subfolder /gateway/PowerShellScript\nRequerements:\n- PowerShell 7+\n### The setup-configuration\nFirst use the Gateway Config Notebook to generate the configuration script for the PowerShell application.\nDownload the config.json created in the “Built-in Resources” of the notebook and create a folder \"/configs/\" in the scripts root folder and copy the JSON file.\nExecute the Setup-UpdateConfiguration Script.\nThe script will first ask you whether you still need to install the necessary PowerShell Modules needed for Lakehouse connectivity (Az.Accounts, Az.Storage, DataGateway).\n...\nOnce the modules have been set, the script will automatically retrieve the Gateway ID and set up the connections to the Eventstream and Lakehouse.\n### Run-GatewayHeartbeat Script:\nThe heartbeat logs contain the status of a gateway.\nThe script will loop and send the logs to the Eventstream to be processed in Fabric.\n### Run-UploadGatewayLogs Script\nThis is the main script that does the data movement from local to the service.\nIn case of the Report files we can send the files to the Eventstream and Lakehouse.\nThe log files are sent to the Lakehouse.\n### Get-DataGatewayInfo\nIt will get the Gateway Node info, we can run this once per week or even lower rate.\n### Schedule the Scripts\nWe can use the Task Scheduler in Windows to automate the script.\nYou will fin a template of the Task Schedulers in the folder \\TaskSchedulers\n## Power BI Gateway Report (Optional)\nUse the template in the \\Gateway Monitoring.pbit and put the parameters: -KustoURL: The Query URL found in the Eventhouse -KustoDB: The name of the KQL DB\nThen load the report and use the credentials with access to the Eventhouse.","date":"2024-05-16"},{"url":"https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","title":"RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"RuiRomano / **\npbigtwmonitor ** Public\n# RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nThis project aim to help organizations with multiple gateway clusters centralize all their gateway logs and reports into a central storage (ADLS Gen 2) and allow easy and quick exploration of those logs either by:\n- Easily access all the gateway logs without having to remote access to the gateway server\n- Explore the logs with a Power BI Report\n- Explore the logs using a SPARK Engine like Azure Synapse Analytics\n**To quickly analyze the logs of gateways you dont need to run/deploy the powershell scripts** its possible to use the disk Power BI template and directly point to the exported gateway logs.\nThe powershell & central storage is only required if you want to keep an fully automated gateway monitoring solution with history log data retention.\n...\n## Deploy scripts to Gateway Server\nOn each Gateway Server you should clone/copy this repo powershell scripts into a local folder (ex: c:\\PBIGTWMonitor)\n...\n# Power BI Template\nThere are two Power BI templates available:\n- Lake Template - When gateway log data resides in the ADLS Gen 2 lake\n- Disk Template - When gateway log data reside in disk, useful to analyze the logs directly from the gateway server or gateway log export.\nYou dont need to deploy the powershell scripts to use this template, only need to have access to gateway logs.","date":"2025-12-10"},{"url":"https://github.com/TALASSX/How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.","title":"GitHub - TALASSX/How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.: The script retrieves all the gateway details for which you have access, then iterates over those gateways to fetch the status, name, and other details. To capture this data every hour, I've set up a Power Automate Desktop flow to execute the PowerShell script, and the output is stored in an Excel sheet on OneDrive.","domain":"github.com","summary":"TALASSX / **\nHow-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.\n** Public\n...\nThe script retrieves all the gateway details for which you have access,\nthen iterates over those gateways to fetch the status, name, and other details.\nTo capture this data every hour,\nI've set up a Power Automate Desktop flow to execute the PowerShell script, and the output is stored in an Excel sheet on OneDrive.\nI hope this project helps you in capturing the on-premises gateway status from Power BI Service.","date":"2023-11-08"},{"url":"https://github.com/martinskeem/powerbi-powershell","title":"martinskeem/powerbi-powershell - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"Various scripts and functions for harvesting Power BI meta data and automating Power BI tasks using API.\n...\n### Report gateway status to event log\nA typical production requirement might be to implement some sort of surveillance of the gateway.\nBelow sample will report current activity state of gateway (active, inactive) into the Application Event Log, which then, in turn could be monitored by a central function.\nBefore the first time the script is executed, the event log source must be created using:\n```\nNew-EventLog -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -LogName \"Application\"\n```\nOnce the source is created, below can be scheduled to run e.g. every 5 minutes after filling in the gateway name and details of the Azure AD app.\n```\n$gatewayName = \"\" # insert name of Gateway to check here.\n$clientId = \"\" # The ID of the Application ID of the App Registration in Azure AD\n$redirectUri = \"\" # One of the redirect URIs provided in the App Registration in Azure AD\n$authHeader = Get-AuthorizationHeader -ClientId $clientId -RedirectUri $redirectUri\n$gateways = Get-Gateways -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader | Where-Object -Property \"name\" -EQ $gatewayName\nif($gateways.Count -eq 0) {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Error -Message \"Gateway '$gatewayName' does not exist.\"\n}\n$gateways | ForEach-Object {\n$gateway = Get-Gateway -GatewayID $_.id -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader\n$annotation = $gateway.gatewayAnnotation | ConvertFrom-Json\nif ($gateway.gatewayStatus -eq \"Live\") {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Information -Message \"Status of '$($gateway.name)' is '$($gateway.gatewayStatus)'.\nRunning on '$($annotation.gatewayMachine)'.\nVersion '$($annotation.gatewayVersion)'\"\n} else {\n...\n}\n}\n...\nVarious scripts and functions for harvesting Power BI meta data and automating Power BI tasks using API.","date":"2017-11-29"},{"url":"https://github.com/kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring","title":"kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring: Templates for monitoring Power BI ...","domain":"github.com","summary":"kerski / **\npbi-dataops-monitoring ** Public\n...\nTemplates for monitoring Power BI components and related Power Platform elements for your data analytics projects.\n...\nEmbracing the DataOps principle \"Monitor Quality and Performance\", this repository provides a set of templates and instructions for monitoring your Power BI solution/projects.\n...\nThe custom data connectors will need to be installed in the a Power BI Gateway in order to refresh the monitoring template in the Power BI Service.\nFor more information on installing a custom data connector with a gateway please see: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-gateway-custom-connectors.\n## Installation\n1. Download the appropriate .pbit file from the latest Release section.\n2. Open the .pbit template.\n3. You will see a prompt as shown in the Figure 1\n*Figure 1 - Example of Template Prompt*\n1. In the field \"Workspace(s) Query\" enter the OData query that identifies the workspace or workspaces you wish to monitor.\nFor example if the workspace you wish to monitor is named 'Test1' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1'\".\nFor another example if the workspaces you wish to monitor are named 'Test1' and 'Test2' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1' or name eq 'Test2'\".\n1. Select the \"Load\" button.\nIf this is your first time using the custom data connectors you will be prompted to sign into Office 365.\nPlease follow the instructions to sign in and then choose the \"Connect\" button.\n*Figure 2 - Sign In Prompt*\n1. and the template will load.\n*Figure 3 - Example of Refresh In Progress* 6.\nWhen completed you should see the Home tab of the monitoring report.\n*Figure 4 - Example of Power BI Monitoring Report*\n1. Please save this file.\nIf you do not have a gateway installed, you will need to refresh this file locally to pull the latest information.\n...\nSince the connector uses the OAuth of the logged in accountto get the Power Automate flows, it's possible that you do not want to monitor all the Power Automate flows that are retrieved by the template.\nTo specify the Power Automate flows you wish to monitor, within the template follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Power Automate Flows\".\n3. Filter the \"Name\" and specify with flows you wish to monitor.\n*Figure 5 - Screenshot of where to filter the Power Automate flows that you wish to monitor.*\n## Exceptions with unscheduled Power BI Datasets\nIf there are datasets that do not require an automated refresh scheduled with the Power BI Dataset settings, you can add those datasets to an exception list.\nWithin the template, please follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Refresh Exception List\"\n3. Within the \"Query Settings\" pane, click the cog icon next to the \"Source\" step.\n*Figure 6 - Screenshot of where to add/update datasets on the refresh exception list.*\n1. Add/Update the names of the datasets you wish to have an exception.\nWhen complete, click the \"OK\" button.\nPlease note the name of the dataset must match **exactly** the name in the Power BI service.","date":"2023-03-24"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance.md","title":"data-integration/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance.md at main · MicrosoftDocs/data-integration","domain":"github.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n:::image type=\"content\" source=\"media/service-gateway-performance/gateway-folder-path-pop-up.png\" alt-text=\"Pop-up for the folder path.\":::\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://github.com/becheng/pbi-azuredevops-poc-pt2","title":"GitHub - becheng/pbi-azuredevops-poc-pt2: Part 2 to the PowerBI + Azure Devops Proof of Concept","domain":"github.com","summary":"Powershell is used to provision the gateway, however at the time of this writing, the feature is under preview so if your preference is to use a user account, follow this msdoc instead.\n1. Download the gateway.ps1 script to the local machine where your on-premise data source resides.\n2. Update the variables within the script including the values you captured for your service principal.\n3. Run the script, e.g.\n   ```\n./gateway.ps1\n```\nand check your Task Manager to confirm the gateway is running.\n4. Sign in to your Power BI portal with your user account.\nGo to Managed Gateways (*Settings* > *Manage Gateways*) and verify your named gateway is listed in *Gateway Clusters*.\n5. Select the gateway and click *Add Data Source* (located at the top).\n6. Name the data source, e.g.\n   ```\nmy-gateway-datasource\n```\nand specify the connection values to the local data source.\n7. Make note of your gateway name as it will be used later.\n...\n1. Sign into your Azure Devops instance and create a new project, e.g.\n   ```\nmy-pbidevops-pipeline\n```\n.\n2. Add your .pbix files to the project's repo.\n3. Download a copy of the deploy-report-with-gateway.ps1 or deploy-pbixreport.ps1 (if using cloud data sources) and upload it to your project's repo.\n#### 5.1 Create a Pipeline\n1. Create a new *Pipeline*, e.g.\n   ```\nmy-pbidevops-build\n```\n.\n2. Copy the following yaml script and save it to the pipeline.\nThis script publishes the .pbix and .ps1 files so the (deployment) *Release* pipeline has access to them.\n```\ntrigger:\n- master\npool:\nvmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'\nsteps:\n- task: CopyFiles@2\ndisplayName: 'Copy Files to: Staging Artifact'\ninputs:\nContents: |\n**/*.pbix\n**/*.ps1\nTargetFolder: '$(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)'\nOverWrite: true\n- task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1\ndisplayName: 'Publish Artifact: drop'\n```\n3. Save and run the build pipeline.\n#### 5.2 Create a Release Pipeline\n1. Create a new *Release* pipeline, e.g.\n   ```\nmy-pbidevops-release\n```\n.\n2. Add an artifact and choose the newly created build pipeline source.\n3. Add a new Stage with an *Empty Job* and provide a name, e.g.\n   ```\nDeploy PBI Report\n```\n.\n4. Click on the \"+\" (*Add a task to the Agent Job*), search and add a *Powershell Task*.\n5. Name the task, e.g.\n   ```\nInstall PS Modules\n```\nand copy and paste the content below as an *Inline* script.\nThis will install the Powershell cmdlets used by the deployment Powershell scripts.\n```\nInstall-Module -Name MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt.Profile -Verbose -Scope CurrentUser -Force\nInstall-Module -Name MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt.Workspaces -Verbose -Scope CurrentUser -Force\nInstall-Module -Name MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt.Reports -Verbose -Scope CurrentUser -Force\nInstall-Module -Name MicrosoftPowerBIMgmt.Data -Verbose -Scope CurrentUser -Force\n```\n6. Click on the \"+\" (*Add a task to the Agent Job*) again and add another *Powershell Task*.\n7. Name the task, e.g.\n   ```\nRun PS deploy script\n```\n, select a *File Path* type, click the elipses *...* and select either the *deploy-pbixreport-with-gateway.ps1* or *deploy-pbixreport.ps1* file.\nNote: Path is visible only if the build pipeline from section 3 ran successfully.\n8. Save the Release.\n...\n4. Save the group and go back to the Release, edit it, select *Variables*, *Variable groups*, and select *Link variable group* and link the variable group to the stage.\n5. Click *Link* to the save linkage.\n...\n1. Click *Tasks* in your pipeline, click on the *Run PS deploy script* and select its *Environment Variables* section.\n2. Enter the following to decrypt all our secret variables:\n|Name|Value|\n|--|--|\n|clientSecret|$(clientSecret)|\n...\n3. Save the Release.\n### 6.0 Running it end to end\n1. Run the Build pipeline.\n2. Run the Release pipeline.\n3. Sign into your Power BI portal using the admin user account and confirm your new workspace is deployed with the report and its dataset is configured correctly (e.g. gateway connection, data source credentials, database parameters, scheduled refresh).","date":"2020-09-08"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/powerbi-docs/blob/main/powerbi-docs/connect-data/service-gateway-onprem-tshoot.md","title":"service-gateway-onprem-tshoot.md - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"Within the Advanced connection properties, include EffectiveUserName for the user in question and see if this addition reproduces the error.\n2. You can use the dsacls Active Directory tool to validate whether the attribute is listed.\n...\n2. After you have the value being passed, validate that it's correct.\nIf it's your user, you can use the following command from a command prompt to see the UPN.\nThe UPN looks like an email address.\n...\nOptionally, you can see what Power BI gets from Microsoft Entra ID.\n1. Browse to https://developer.microsoft.com/graph/graph-explorer .\n2. Select **Sign in** in the upper-right corner.\n3. Run the following query.\nYou see a rather large JSON response.\n```\n...\n```\n4. Look for **userPrincipalName**.\n...\nUse the following steps to extract these logs.\n1. Identify the gateway cluster.\nIf you're a dataset owner, first check the gateway cluster name associated with your dataset.\nIn the following image, *IgniteGateway* is the gateway cluster.\n2. Check the gateway properties.\nThe gateway admin should then check the number of gateway members in the cluster and if load balancing is enabled.\nIf load balancing is enabled, then step 3 should be repeated for all gateway members.\nIf it's not enabled, then exporting logs on the primary gateway is sufficient.\n3. Retrieve and export the gateway logs.\nNext, the gateway admin, who is also the administrator of the gateway system, should do the following steps:\na.\nSign in to the gateway machine, and then launch the on-premises data gateway app to sign in to the gateway.\n   b. Enable additional logging .\n   c. Optionally, you can enable the performance monitoring features and include performance logs to provide additional details for troubleshooting.\n   d. Run the scenario for which you're trying to capture gateway logs.\n   e. Export the gateway logs .","date":"2018-01-25"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"site:github.com on-premises data gateway PowerShell health","limit":8},"result":[{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/azure-docs/blob/main/articles/logic-apps/multi-region-disaster-recovery.md","title":"Multi-region deployments for disaster recovery in Azure Logic Apps","domain":"github.com","summary":"- On-premises data gateways which are Azure resources that you create and use in your logic apps to access data in on-premises systems.\nEach gateway resource represents a separate data gateway installation on a local computer.\n...\n## On-premises data gateways\nIf your logic app runs in multitenant Azure and needs access to on-premises resources such as SQL Server databases, you need to install the on-premises data gateway on a local computer.\nYou can then create a data gateway resource in the Azure portal so that your logic app can use the gateway when you create a connection to the resource.\nThe data gateway resource is associated with a location or Azure region, just like your logic app resource.\nIn your multi-region redundancy strategy, make sure that the data gateway remains available for your logic app to use.\nYou can enable high availability for your gateway when you have multiple gateway installations.\n...\nFor a multi-region failover strategy to work, your solution needs ways to perform these tasks:\n- Check the primary instance's availability\n- Monitor the primary instance's health\n- Activate the secondary instance\n...\nTo determine whether the primary instance is available, running, and able to work, you can create a \"health-check\" logic app that's in the same location as the primary instance.\nYou can then call this health-check app from an alternate location.\nIf the health-check app successfully responds, the underlying infrastructure for the Azure Logic Apps service in that region is available and working.\nIf the health-check app fails to respond, you can assume that the location is no longer healthy.\nFor this task, create a basic health-check logic app that performs these tasks:\n1. Receives a call from the watchdog app by using the Request trigger.\n2. Respond with a status indicating whether the checked logic app still works by using the Response action.\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The health-check logic app must use a Response action so that the app responds synchronously, not asynchronously.\n3. Optionally, to further determine whether the primary location is healthy, you can factor in the health of any other services that interact with the target logic app in this location.\nJust expand the health-check logic app to assess the health for these other services too.\n...\nTo monitor the primary instance's health and call the health-check logic app, create a \"watchdog\" logic app in an *alternate location*.\nFor example, you can set up the watchdog logic app so that if calling the health-check logic fails, the watchdog can send an alert to your operations team so that they can investigate the failure and why the primary instance doesn't respond.\n...\nMake sure that your watchdog logic app is in a *location that differs from primary location*.\nIf Azure Logic Apps in the primary location experiences problems, your watchdog logic app workflow might not run.\nFor this task, in the secondary location, create a watchdog logic app that performs these tasks:\n1. Run based on a fixed or scheduled recurrence by using the Recurrence trigger.\nYou can set the recurrence to a value that below the tolerance level for your recovery time objective (RTO).\n2. Call the health-check logic app workflow in the primary location by using the HTTP action.\n...\nTo automatically activate the secondary instance, you can create a logic app that calls the management API such as the Azure Resource Manager connector to activate the appropriate logic apps in the secondary location.\nYou can expand your watchdog app to call this activation logic app after a specific number of failures happen.\n...\nYou can set up logging for your logic app runs and send the resulting diagnostic data to services such as Azure Storage, Azure Event Hubs, and Azure Log Analytics for further handling and processing.\n- If you want to use this data with Azure Log Analytics, you can make the data available for both the primary and secondary locations by setting up your logic app's **Diagnostic settings** and sending the data to multiple Log Analytics workspaces.","date":"2016-11-02"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-powershell-support.md","title":"data-integration/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-powershell-support.md at main · MicrosoftDocs/data-integration","domain":"github.com","summary":"|PowerShell support for on-premises data gateway clusters|You can retrieve gateway and cluster information, modify the status within a gateway, or delete a gateway by using PowerShell commands.|dougklo|conceptual|05/06/2024|\n...\nPowerShell scripts are available in the PowerShell gallery.\nThe scripts described in this article are for PowerShell version 5.\nWhen you use PowerShell version 7, refer to PowerShell Cmdlets for on-premises data gateway management.\nYou can use the PowerShell scripts to perform the following operations:\n- Retrieve the list of gateway clusters available for a user.\n- Retrieve the list of gateway instances registered in a cluster and their online or offline status.\n- Modify the enable or disable status for a gateway instance within a cluster and other gateway properties.\n- Delete a gateway.\n## Run the PowerShell commands\nTo install these cmdlets, run the following command in an elevated PowerShell session:\n```\nInstall-Module -Name OnPremisesDataGatewayHAMgmt\n```\nThe entire list of cmdlets can be found using the following command:\n```\nGet-Command -Module OnPremisesDataGateway*\n```\nExamples and descriptions are included in the cmdlets and you can access them using the following command:\n```\nget-help <cmdlet-name>\n```\nNow you can use the commands in the following table to manage your gateway clusters.\n|**Command**|**Description**|**Parameters**|\n|--|--|--|\n|*Login-OnPremisesDataGateway*|Use this command to sign in to manage your on-premises data gateway clusters.\nYou must run this command and sign in *before* other high-availability commands can work properly.\nNote: The Microsoft Entra ID auth token acquired as part of a `login` call is valid for only 1 hour, after which it expires.\nYou can rerun the `login` command to acquire a new token.|Microsoft Entra ID username and password (provided as part of the command execution, not initial invocation).|\n|*Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayClusters*|Retrieves the list of gateway clusters for the signed-in user.|Optionally, you can pass formatting parameters to this command for better readability, such as *Format-Table -AutoSize -Wrap*.|\n|*Get-OnPremisesDataClusterGateways*|Retrieves the list of gateways within the specified cluster and additional information for each gateway like online or offline status and machine name.|*-ClusterObjectID xyz* (where *xyz* is replaced with an actual cluster object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayClusters* command).|\n|*Set-OnPremisesDataGateway*|Use this command to set property values for a given gateway within a cluster, which includes the ability to enable or disable a specific gateway instance.|*-ClusterObjectID xyz* (where *xyz* is replaced with an actual cluster object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayClusters* command).\n*-GatewayObjectID abc* (where *abc* is replaced with an actual gateway object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataClusterGateways* command, given a cluster object ID).|\n|*Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayStatus*|Use this command to retrieve the status for a given gateway instance within a cluster.|*-ClusterObjectID xyz* (where *xyz* is replaced with an actual cluster object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayClusters* command).\n*-GatewayObjectID abc* (where *abc* is replaced with an actual gateway object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataClusterGateways* command, given a cluster object ID).|\n|*Remove-OnPremisesDataGateway*|Use this command to remove a gateway instance from a cluster.\nNote: The primary gateway in the cluster can't be removed until all other gateways in the cluster are removed.|*-ClusterObjectID xyz* (where *xyz* is replaced with an actual cluster object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataGatewayClusters* command).\n*-GatewayObjectID abc* (where *abc* is replaced with an actual gateway object ID value, which can be retrieved by using the *Get-OnPremisesDataClusterGateways* command, given a cluster object ID).|\n...\n- Remove or delete an on-premises data gateway","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/powerbi-docs/blob/main/powerbi-docs/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-data-gateways.md","title":"powerbi-implementation-planning-data-gateways.md - GitHub","domain":"github.com","date":"2018-01-25"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/azure-docs/blob/main/articles/analysis-services/analysis-services-gateway-install.md","title":"azure-docs/articles/analysis-services/analysis-services-gateway-install.md at main · MicrosoftDocs/azure-docs","domain":"github.com","summary":"Run setup.\nSelect\n**On-premises data gateway**.\n...\n**Install**.\nSign in to Azure.\n...\nIn order to create a gateway resource in Azure, you must register the local instance you installed with the Gateway Cloud Service.\nSelect\n**Register a new gateway on this computer**.\nType a name and recovery key for your gateway.\nBy default, the gateway uses your subscription's default region.\nIf you need to select a different region, select\n**Change Region**.\n...\nAfter you've installed and registered your gateway, you need to create a gateway resource in Azure.\nSign in to Azure with the same account you used when registering the gateway.\nIn Azure portal, click\n**Create a resource**, then search for **On-premises data gateway**, and then click **Create**.\nIn\n**Create connection gateway**, enter these settings:\n**Name**: Enter a name for your gateway resource.\n**Subscription**: Select the Azure subscription to associate with your gateway resource.\nThe default subscription is based on the Azure account that you used to sign in.\n**Resource group**: Create a resource group or select an existing resource group.\n**Location**: Select the region you registered your gateway in.\n**Installation Name**: If your gateway installation isn't already selected, select the gateway you installed on your computer and registered.\nWhen you're done, click\n**Create**.\n...\nConnecting to a gateway resource in a different subscription from your server is not supported in the portal, but is supported using PowerShell.\n# Portal\nIn your Azure Analysis Services server overview, click\n**On-Premises Data Gateway**.\nIn\n**Pick an On-Premises Data Gateway to connect**, select your gateway resource, and then click **Connect selected gateway**.\n[!NOTE] If your gateway does not appear in the list, your server is likely not in the same region as the region you specified when registering the gateway.\nWhen connection between your server and gateway resource is successful, status will show\n**Connected**.\n# PowerShell\nUse Get-AzResource to get the gateway ResourceID.\nThen connect the gateway resource to an existing or new server by specifying\n**-GatewayResourceID** in Set-AzAnalysisServicesServer or New-AzAnalysisServicesServer.\nTo get the gateway resource ID:\n```\nConnect-AzAccount -Tenant $TenantId -Subscription $subscriptionIdforGateway -Environment \"AzureCloud\"\n$GatewayResourceId = $(Get-AzResource -ResourceType \"Microsoft.Web/connectionGateways\" -Name $gatewayName).ResourceId\n```\nTo configure an existing server:\n```\nConnect-AzAccount -Tenant $TenantId -Subscription $subscriptionIdforAzureAS -Environment \"AzureCloud\"\nSet-AzAnalysisServicesServer -ResourceGroupName $RGName -Name $servername -GatewayResourceId $GatewayResourceId\n```\n...\nBy default, the gateway will spool data before sending it to the Analysis Services server resource, potentially causing slower performance during data load and refresh operations.\nTo ensure optimal performance, override the default setting:\nIn the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\\n**Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config**file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes**setting to **True**, and then save.\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\"> <value>True</value> </setting>\nIn\n**On-premises data gateway**> **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2016-11-02"},{"url":"https://github.com/joeshen117/connectionCheckerScript","title":"GitHub - joeshen117/connectionCheckerScript: A PowerShell script you can run to check all On-premises Data Gateways used by API connections","domain":"github.com","summary":"# On-premises Data Gateway checker script\n**This repository contains a PowerShell script you can run to list all On-premises Data Gateways used by Logic App API connections and save to a CSV file.** For more information on finding correlations between Logic App API connections and On-premises Data Gateways, please visit my blog here.\n...\nFind out which On-premises Data Gateways are used by which API connection resources.\n...\nAfter downloading the script into your machine, put in the name of your Azure subscription for the **subscriptionName** variable.\nThen run the script and log into your Azure account in the pop-up window.\nA CSV file named **“output.csv”** will be created in the same folder where the script resides.\n...\nA PowerShell script you can run to check all On-premises Data Gateways used by API connections\n### Topics","date":"2021-04-14"},{"url":"https://github.com/TALASSX/How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.","title":"GitHub - TALASSX/How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.: The script retrieves all the gateway details for which you have access, then iterates over those gateways to fetch the status, name, and other details. To capture this data every hour, I've set up a Power Automate Desktop flow to execute the PowerShell script, and the output is stored in an Excel sheet on OneDrive.","domain":"github.com","summary":"How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using-PowerShell-and-Power-Automate.\n** Public\n...\nThe script retrieves all the gateway details for which you have access,\nthen iterates over those gateways to fetch the status, name, and other details.\nTo capture this data every hour,\nI've set up a Power Automate Desktop flow to execute the PowerShell script, and the output is stored in an Excel sheet on OneDrive.\nI hope this project helps you in capturing the on-premises gateway status from Power BI Service.","date":"2023-11-08"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-app.md","title":"data-integration/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-app.md at main · MicrosoftDocs/data-integration","domain":"github.com","summary":"To open the on-premises data gateway app:\n1. On the machine where the gateway is running, enter **gateway** in Windows search.\n2. Select the **On-premises data gateway** app.\n...\n|Status|Status of the gateway cluster|Indicates whether your gateway is online, the version number of the gateway, and a list of any apps currently associated with the gateway.|","date":"2019-07-16"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/main/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-monthly-updates.md","title":"data-integration/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-monthly-updates.md at main · MicrosoftDocs/data-integration","domain":"github.com","date":"2019-07-16"}],"error":null}],"total":10,"saved_to":"/home/user/.pplx/search/pplx_sdk_2026-06-30T23-33-46.773894Z_47a3b4b5.json"}
